.TH CONDUITSCOPE 1 "2026" "conduitscope 0.2.4" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
conduitscope \- decode Modbus/TCP, DNP3, IEC 60870-5-104, S7comm/COTP, S7comm-Plus (Siemens TIA Portal / S7-1200/1500), IEC 61850 MMS (Manufacturing Message Specification, ISO 9506), EtherNet/IP (CIP explicit and implicit messaging), PROFINET RT (DCP and cyclic real-time IO), IEC 61850-8-1 GOOSE, IEC 61850-9-2 Sampled Values, EtherCAT, BACnet/IP, HART-IP, OPC UA Binary, MQTT (v3.1/v3.1.1/v5.0, including Sparkplug B), FOUNDATION Fieldbus HSE (FDA/SM/FMS/LAN Redundancy), IEEE Spanning Tree Protocol (STP/RSTP/MSTP), DeviceNet (CAN-bus CIP, via SocketCAN pcap captures), Kerberos, LDAP, SMB2/NTLM (the Windows Active Directory suite), DNS, mDNS, LLMNR, and NetBIOS Name Service (NBT-NS), RIP, IGMP, VRRP, HSRP, IGRP, PIM, EIGRP, and OSPFv2, plus detect DNS-over-HTTPS (DoH) via TLS SNI matching, plus recognize (by name only, not full decode) RDP, VNC, TeamViewer, AnyDesk, and Zoom, plus recognize (by name only, and for SNMP also its cleartext community string) SSH, HTTP, HTTPS, SNMPv1/v2c, Telnet, FTP, and TFTP, plus recognize (by name only, and for DHCP also its message type) NTP, DHCP, LDAPS, RADIUS, TACACS+, and IEEE 802.1X/EAPOL, plus recognize (by name only, and for CAPWAP control and GTP-U also their message type) CAPWAP control/data, LWAPP control/data, GTP-U, and PPPoE, plus recognize (by name only, except for IP-in-IP's own inner src/dst addresses) GRE (and its NVGRE/Mikrotik EoIP sub-cases), IPsec ESP/AH, IP-in-IP, 6in4, L2TP/L2TPv3, IKE, VXLAN, Geneve, WireGuard, OpenVPN, a generic DTLS-tunnel structural check, STT, and MPLS, from offline pcap/pcapng captures
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B conduitscope
[\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-color\fR|\fB\-\-color\fR] [\fB\-\-log\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B conduitscope
reads a pcap or pcapng capture file, or optionally a live network
interface (see \fBLIVE CAPTURE\fR below), and decodes Modbus/TCP, DNP3,
IEC 60870-5-104 (TCP port 2404), S7comm (Siemens S7 PLC protocol, over
TPKT/COTP, TCP port 102, including PLC Control/PLC Stop parameter
decoding), S7comm-Plus (Siemens TIA Portal / S7-1200/1500's
newer, object-oriented protocol, the same TPKT/COTP transport and TCP port
102 S7comm uses but disambiguated by its own protocol id byte, 0x72 vs
S7comm's 0x32 -- GetMultiVariables/SetMultiVariables/SetVariable/
DeleteObject fully decoded, everything else named but not body-decoded),
IEC 61850 MMS (Manufacturing Message
Specification, ISO 9506, the same TPKT/COTP transport and TCP port 102
S7comm uses, but a distinct, independent application protocol with its own
Session/Presentation/ACSE layering -- detected by its own separate
structural gate, tried once S7comm's own gate has already failed), and
EtherNet/IP (CIP explicit messaging, TCP port
44818) traffic found inside its TCP streams, plus EtherNet/IP CIP I/O
(implicit messaging, UDP port 2222) traffic found inside its UDP streams,
plus Beckhoff TwinCAT/ADS (AMS/TCP, TCP port 48898, tried right after
Modbus using a five-part structural gate -- see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's TwinCAT / ADS
section) traffic found inside its TCP streams,
plus MELSEC Communication Protocol (MC Protocol / SLMP, Mitsubishi
Electric, TCP port 5001 and UDP port 5000, tried right after IEC 104 but
BEFORE Modbus on TCP -- a real MELSEC-vs-Modbus collision was found post-
delivery and fixed by this ordering -- and right after BACnet/IP and
EtherNet/IP CIP I/O but BEFORE HART-IP on UDP -- a two-part structural
gate, exact 3E/4E subheader magic plus an
exact declared-length cross-check -- see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's
MELSEC / MC Protocol section for the wire format, the collision surveys
against Modbus's own weaker TCP gate and HART-IP's own weaker UDP gate,
and its curated unauthenticated-
CPU-control/cleartext-password-field/arbitrary-memory-access attack-
monitoring notes; the cleartext Remote Password field's own value is
never rendered, only its length) traffic found inside its TCP and UDP
streams,
plus FINS (Factory Interface Network Service, Omron, TCP port 9600 and
UDP port 9600, tried right after MELSEC on both transports -- a stronger
gate than Modbus's own on TCP thanks to its exact 4-byte "FINS" magic, and
deliberately BEFORE HART-IP on UDP for the same reason MELSEC is, since
HART-IP's own weak UDP gate has a real, partial overlap with FINS's own
byte layout too -- see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's FINS section for
the wire format, the collision surveys, and its curated unauthenticated-
CPU-control/arbitrary-memory-access/forced-I/O-override attack-monitoring
notes) traffic found inside its TCP and UDP streams,
plus Kerberos (RFC 4120, TCP and UDP port 88, tried right after TwinCAT on
TCP and right after HART-IP on UDP, recognized by its outer ASN.1
APPLICATION tag byte plus a pvno/msg-type cross-check on full decode --
see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's Kerberos section for the wire format
and its curated AS-REP-Roasting/Kerberoasting attack-monitoring notes)
traffic found inside its TCP and UDP streams,
plus LDAP (RFC 4511, TCP port 389 and TCP port 3268 Global Catalog, tried
right after Kerberos on TCP, no UDP sibling -- IMPLICIT ASN.1 tagging,
the opposite of Kerberos's own EXPLICIT convention, see
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's LDAP section for the wire format and
its curated anonymous-bind/cleartext-credential/AD-reconnaissance/
AS-REP-Roasting/delegation-discovery attack-monitoring notes; replaces
the shallow, name-only LDAP recognition \fB\-\-protocol enterprise\-trust\fR
used to provide) traffic found inside its TCP streams,
plus SMB2/NTLM (MS-SMB2/MS-NLMP, TCP port 445 and TCP port 139, tried
right after LDAP on TCP, no UDP sibling -- recognized by the same 4-byte
\fB0xFF\fR/\fB0xFE\fR/\fB0xFD\fR + "SMB" magic \fB\-\-protocol
lateral\-movement\fR used to provide, now additionally requiring the
4-byte Zero+StreamProtocolLength Direct-TCP/NBSS prefix on both ports --
see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's SMB2/NTLM section for the wire
format, NextCommand compounding, the shared NTLM (MS-NLMP)
negotiate/challenge/authenticate parser, and its curated SMB1-present/
signing-not-required/NTLM-in-use/anonymous-or-guest-session/
administrative-share-access/repeated-logon-failure attack-monitoring
notes; replaces the shallow, name-only SMB recognition \fB\-\-protocol
lateral\-movement\fR used to provide) traffic found inside its TCP
streams, plus Netlogon/DCE-RPC (MS-NRPC/MS-RPCE, no independent wire
gate or port/flag of its own -- recognized structurally, inside SMB2
WRITE/READ/IOCTL against a tracked \fB\\PIPE\\netlogon\fR named-pipe
handle -- see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's Netlogon/DCE-RPC section
for the wire format and its curated secure-channel-established/
all-zero-challenge-or-credential (the CVE-2020-1472 "Zerologon" wire
signature)/legacy-authentication-method/machine-account-naming-mismatch/
password-reset attack-monitoring notes) traffic found inside those SMB2
messages,
plus SAMR/LSARPC (MS-SAMR, MS-LSAD/MS-LSAT, the same no-independent-gate
shape as Netlogon/DCE-RPC above, recognized inside SMB2 WRITE/READ against
tracked \fB\\PIPE\\samr\fR/\fB\\PIPE\\lsarpc\fR named-pipe handles -- see
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's SAMR + LSARPC section for the wire
format and its curated account/group-enumeration, SID/name-translation-
or-enumeration, cross-interface, and null/guest-session-escalation
attack-monitoring notes) traffic found inside those SMB2 messages,
plus SRVSVC/WKSSVC (MS-SRVS, MS-WKST, the same no-independent-gate shape,
recognized inside SMB2 WRITE/READ against tracked \fB\\PIPE\\srvsvc\fR/
\fB\\PIPE\\wkssvc\fR named-pipe handles -- see
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's SRVSVC + WKSSVC section for the wire
format, its level-1-scoped share-enumeration/workstation-identity/logged-
on-user decode, and its curated share-added/deleted and domain-join/
unjoin attack-monitoring notes) traffic found inside those SMB2 messages,
plus DRSUAPI (MS-DRSR, the same no-independent-gate shape, recognized
inside SMB2 WRITE/READ against a tracked \fB\\PIPE\\drsuapi\fR named-pipe
handle -- a synthetic gate name this codebase invented, matching its own
per-interface convention, since MS-DRSR names no well-known named pipe at
all; real-world DRSUAPI traffic, including DCSync, predominantly rides a
dynamically negotiated raw TCP connection via the RPC endpoint mapper
instead, which this decoder does NOT follow -- see
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's DRSUAPI section, and especially its own
"critical, empirically-discovered scope caveat" subsection, before relying
on this decoder for DCSync detection) traffic found inside those SMB2
messages, with its curated DRSGetNCChanges/DCSync-signature
attack-monitoring note (fires even under RPC-layer sealing, since the
opnum itself is never encrypted),
plus WinRM (WS-Management, MS-WSMV \-\- TCP port 5985, plaintext only; port
5986/TLS out of scope \-\- a minimal HTTP/1.1 + SOAP 1.2 parser built from
scratch for this protocol, genuinely different from every DCE/RPC
interface above: no \fBsmb.hpp\fR named pipe, no interface UUID) traffic
found on that port, decoding a shell session's own
Create/Command/Send/Receive/Signal/Delete exchanges (ShellId/CommandId
correlated across messages) \-\- see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's
WinRM section, and especially its own "critical, empirically-corrected
wire-format detail" subsection (the command line is plain XML text, NOT
base64+UTF-16LE as this codebase's own original implementation plan
assumed), before relying on this decoder's own \fBwinrm_command_line\fR
field, with its curated remote-shell-opened/command-executed (the command
line redacted by default, \fB\-\-no\-redact\fR opt-in)/CIM-WMI-query-over-
WinRM/PowerShell-Remoting-endpoint/HTTP-Basic-auth-over-plaintext/SOAP-
Fault attack-monitoring notes,
plus DCOM activation (MS-DCOM \-\- raw DCE/RPC directly over TCP port 135,
NOT SMB-wrapped, reusing the same interface-agnostic DCE/RPC envelope
decoder every SMB-carried interface above shares; structural activation/
OXID-resolution recognition only \-\- \fBIObjectExporter\fR/
\fBIRemoteSCMActivator\fR/\fBIActivation\fR named by interface and opnum,
no request/response body field ever decoded, a still narrower scope than
DRSUAPI's own \-\- see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's DCOM section for
why, including the empirically-corrected \fBIObjectExporter\fR interface
UUID this codebase's own original plan got wrong) traffic found on that
port, with its curated DCOM-activation-observed note (naming interface and
opnum) and its explicit \fBResolveOxid\fR/\fBResolveOxid2\fR scope-boundary
note (the dynamically negotiated data-channel port those calls resolve is
NOT followed),
plus GE SRTP (Service Request Transport Protocol, GE Fanuc/GE Intelligent
Platforms \-\- TCP port 18245, a proprietary programming/monitoring/control
protocol for the 90\-30/90\-70/RX3i/RX7i PLC families, named
\fBge\-srtp\fR/\fBGeSrtp*\fR throughout this codebase, deliberately never
bare "srtp", to avoid confusion with the unrelated IETF Secure Real\-time
Transport Protocol) traffic found on that port, wire\-gated
(\fBGateKind::TcpPort\fR) with authoritative session\-scoped
request/response pairing by GE SRTP's own wire\-carried Sequence Number
(a materially stronger guarantee than MELSEC's/FINS's own
single\-outstanding\-slot heuristic pairing), and curated
unauthenticated\-memory\-read/write, unauthenticated\-controller\-type\-
reconnaissance, program\-store/program\-load, run/stop\-control, and
fault\-table\-access attack\-monitoring notes sourced from the DFRWS 2017
forensics paper's own field\-deployment finding that real GE Fanuc Series
90\-30 installations commonly run this protocol with no protocol\-level
authentication at all \-\- \fBnote:\fR two real, reproducible protocol\-
collision bugs against the existing TPKT/COTP decoder were found and
fixed while building this decoder (GE SRTP's own Packet Type value for
every response happens to supply TPKT's own magic version byte by
coincidence); see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's GE SRTP section for
the full writeup, including the "TWO\-PHASE DECLARATION" fix,
plus BSAP (Bristol Standard Asynchronous/Synchronous Protocol, Bristol
Babcock/Emerson Remote Automation Solutions \-\- UDP port 1234, wire\-gated
(\fBGateKind::UdpPort\fR)) traffic found on that port, decoding link\-layer
framing/addressing on both the serial\-tunneled (magic \fB0x0210\fR,
Local/Global addressing, \fBDFUN\fR/\fBSFUN\fR link\-function decode) and
BSAP\-IP\-native transports, deliberately structural\-only \-\- no RDB
(Remote Database Access) application\-layer semantics are decoded on
either transport, by Jurgen's own explicit choice after a confirmed
sourcing gap left no numeric RDB function\-code table available in any
public source, including CISA's own reference open\-source Zeek parser
\-\- \fBnote:\fR two real, reproducible dispatch\-ordering collision bugs
against the existing HART\-IP and FF\-HSE decoders were found and fixed
while building this decoder; see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's BSAP
section for the full writeup,
plus CC\-Link IE Field Network Basic (CCIEFB, Mitsubishi Electric \-\- UDP
ports 61450 cyclic data / 61451 SLMP node search and Set IP Address),
decoding cyclic I/O data (master/slave station notification, RWw/RWr/RY/RX
byte counts), SLMP node search (discovery/enumeration), and SLMP Set IP
Address (flagged with its own security note \-\- it remotely reassigns a
slave's IP address with no documented credential requirement in any
source); the only CC\-Link IE family member implemented, since CC\-Link IE
Control/Field/TSN require dedicated ASIC hardware with no public UDP/IP
wire documentation. Rides the same SLMP 3E/4E outer framing as MELSEC;
coexistence is handled by a request\-side command\-code gate plus
session\-scoped response matching, designed in proactively (not a post\-hoc
collision fix) and verified against a genuine MELSEC command and an
orphan response in the test fixture; see
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's CC\-Link IE section for the full writeup,
plus a cross\-cutting attack\-detection layer applied to every decoded
IPv4/TCP/UDP/ICMP packet regardless of which application protocol claims
it \-\- LAND, WinNuke, ICMP Redirect, IP Source Routing (LSRR/SSRR), Smurf,
Fraggle, Ping of Death, and Teardrop as curated structural signatures
(sourced against H3C's and Juniper's own attack\-detection documentation),
plus SYN flood, ACK flood, ICMP flood, UDP flood, and a generic TCP\-flood
catch\-all, each a deliberately simple per\-destination whole\-capture packet
count against \fB\-\-flood\-threshold\fR (default 100, a documented judgment
call rather than a vendor\-sourced figure) \-\- \fBnote:\fR two real
false\-positive collisions against the existing BACnet and
HART\-IP/SAMR\-LSARPC fixtures were found and fixed while building this
feature; see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's Attack Detection section for
the full writeup,
plus CODESYS V3 (3S\-Smart/CODESYS GmbH's PLC runtime protocol, licensed
to dozens of PLC vendors including WAGO, Festo, and Eaton \-\- TCP ports
11740/1217 Block Driver\-framed, UDP ports 1740\-1743 unframed), decoding
the full four\-layer wire format (Block Driver, Datagram/Router, Channel,
Services) and CmpDevice's own Login/AUTH exchange in full (username
rendered, password NEVER rendered, matching this tool's own
never\-render\-credential\-bytes convention), with every other CODESYS
service deliberately structural\-only (component/command named, payload
byte count only) by Jurgen's own explicit choice; CODESYS V2 is out of
scope, no public wire\-format documentation found for it; see
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's CODESYS section for the full writeup,
plus CoAP (Constrained Application Protocol, RFC 7252 \-\- a single
authoritative IETF standard, unlike several of this tool's other recent
additions, which needed third\-party reverse\-engineering \-\- UDP port
5683), decoding the base RFC plus Observe (RFC 7641) and blockwise
transfer (RFC 7959) in full, with any other IANA\-registered option
structurally recognized but not value\-decoded; port\-gated in \fBauto\fR
mode, unlike CODESYS/CC\-Link IE above, since CoAP's own shortest legal
message is too weak a structural signal to try opportunistically; see
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's CoAP section for the full writeup,
plus Zigbee (IEEE 802.15.4 MAC + Zigbee NWK + Zigbee APS + full ZDP,
carried over LINKTYPE_IEEE802_15_4_WITHFCS/195 and
LINKTYPE_IEEE802_15_4_TAP/283 pcap captures, sourced from literal
Wireshark dissector C code and treated as authoritative), decoding the
MAC header, NWK layer, and APS layer structurally always \-\- these ride
in the clear even when the payload above them is encrypted \-\- and full
ZDP across 13 named clusters (NWK_addr, IEEE_addr, Node_Desc,
Simple_Desc, Active_EP, Match_Desc, Device_annce, Bind, Unbind,
Mgmt_Lqi, Mgmt_Rtg, Mgmt_Leave, Mgmt_Permit_Joining) whenever APS\-layer
security is not in use; when NWK or APS security IS in use, the
encrypted payload is reported as opaque (\fBnote:\fR "N byte(s) of
NWK/APS\-encrypted payload") rather than guessed at, with no decryption
capability at all; ZCL (Zigbee Cluster Library) and Zigbee Green Power
are explicitly out of scope; see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's
Zigbee section for the full writeup,
plus CDP (Cisco Discovery Protocol, SNAP\-encapsulated over classic IEEE
802.3 LLC framing, Cisco OUI 00:00:0C with SNAP Protocol ID 0x2000),
decoding Device ID, Port ID, Platform, Software Version, Capabilities
(every bit named), Native VLAN, Duplex, Addresses and Management Address
(IPv4 rendered as a dotted quad, other protocol families named only),
VTP Management Domain, System Name, and Power Consumption/Requested/
Available, with every other TLV including HP\-proprietary extensions
named structurally; also fixes a pre\-existing bug where every
Cisco\-OUI SNAP frame, including CDP's own, was mislabeled as PVST+; see
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's CDP section for the full writeup,
plus PROFINET RT (EtherType 0x8892, DCP device discovery/configuration and
cyclic real-time IO), IEC 61850-8-1 GOOSE (EtherType 0x88B8, ASN.1
BER-encoded GOOSE PDU), IEC 61850-9-2 Sampled Values (EtherType 0x88BA,
GOOSE's sibling protocol, ASN.1 BER-encoded SavPdu), and EtherCAT (EtherType
0x88A4, plain fixed-binary-layout framing, no ASN.1/BER) traffic found
directly on the wire, with no
IP/TCP/UDP layer at all, plus BACnet/IP (UDP port 47808/0xBAC0, ASHRAE 135
Annex J, detected by payload shape rather than port) traffic found inside
its UDP streams, plus HART-IP (UDP/TCP port 5094, IEC 62591 / HCF_SPEC-151,
tried last of all protocols on both transports due to its own genuinely
weak structural detection gate) traffic found on either, plus OPC UA Binary
(TCP port 4840, UA-TCP transport / OPC UA Secure Conversation, OPC 10000-6,
tried FIRST of all protocols due to its own strong structural detection
gate) traffic found inside its TCP streams, plus MQTT (v3.1/v3.1.1/v5.0,
conventionally TCP port 1883, tried LAST of all protocols \-\- even after
HART-IP \-\- due to having no fixed magic number or length field of its own)
and, layered on top of it, Sparkplug B (the Eclipse Tahu IIoT convention,
protobuf-encoded payloads under the \fIspBv1.0/...\fR topic namespace,
decoded via a hand-rolled Protocol Buffers reader) traffic found inside its
TCP streams, plus FOUNDATION Fieldbus HSE (FF-HSE \-\- FDA port 1090, SM
port 1091, LAN Redundancy port 3622, ff-annunc port 1089, all TCP AND UDP,
tried dead LAST of all protocols on both transports due to its own
genuinely weak, single-byte structural detection gate \-\- the sub-protocol
itself is signaled in-band by the header, not by port) traffic found on
either transport, plus IEEE Spanning Tree Protocol (STP/RSTP/MSTP \-\-
BPDUs riding classic IEEE 802.3 length-framed Ethernet with a 3-byte LLC
header, DSAP=SSAP=0x42, no IP/TCP/UDP layer and no EtherType at all \-\-
the first protocol here reached neither via IPv4 nor a DIX EtherType, a
structurally separate dispatch path from every EtherType-keyed protocol
above) traffic found directly on the wire, plus DeviceNet (CAN-bus CIP \-\-
a pcap link type of its own, \fILINKTYPE_CAN_SOCKETCAN\fR, rather than
Ethernet at all: no MAC addresses, no IP layer, no EtherType, dispatched
through a wholly separate top-level branch checked before any Ethernet
parsing is attempted) traffic found on a SocketCAN capture, plus DNS/mDNS/
LLMNR (UDP ports 53/5353/5355, one shared RFC 1035-derived wire format) and
NetBIOS Name Service/NBT-NS (UDP port 137, RFC 1002) traffic, port-gated
detection in \fBauto\fR mode (the only protocols here that are, since none
has a self-describing wire-format signal strong enough for this tool's
usual port-independent posture -- see \fBdocs/USER_GUIDE.md\fR's PROTOCOL
DETECTION section), plus DNS-over-HTTPS (DoH) detection (TCP port 443,
also port-gated) via a TLS ClientHello's plaintext SNI matched against a
curated table of known public DoH resolvers -- detection only, since DoH's
actual DNS content is TLS-encrypted and never decoded, plus recognizes (by
name only, port plus a minimal structural signature where one exists --
never full decoding) RDP (TCP port 3389, identified from its initial X.224
Connection Request/Confirm reusing this tool's own TPKT/COTP parser), VNC
(a genuine RFB protocol-version banner, checked port-independently), and
TeamViewer/AnyDesk/Zoom (recognized by port number alone -- no payload
signature exists for any of the three, the weakest identification gate in
this tool), plus recognizes (same name-only posture, Tier 2 of the same
"IT protocols an OT auditor flags" family -- SMB has since been promoted
to a full decoder, see above) SSH (an RFC 4253 version-exchange
banner), HTTP (a request-line/status-line, checked port-independently so a
vendor web UI on any port is still caught), HTTPS (a TLS ClientHello,
reusing the same parser DoH detection above uses), SNMPv1/v2c (UDP ports
161/162, an ASN.1 BER structure whose cleartext community string is
genuinely extracted and surfaced -- the one deliberate departure from this
tool's usual name-only posture), and Telnet/FTP/TFTP (TCP ports 23/21, UDP
port 69, each with a narrower, port-gated structural or fallback signal),
and checks
decoded traffic against a
zone-and-conduit segmentation policy (IEC 62443 / NIS2 style work) via
\fBpolicy validate\fR.
.PP
Both classic pcap (what tcpdump writes by default) and pcapng (what
\fBdumpcap\fR/current Wireshark default to) are read transparently --
auto-detected from the file itself, nothing to specify on the command
line. See \fBdocs/USER_GUIDE.md\fR's "pcap vs. pcapng" section for exactly
which pcapng block types are understood.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B decode (\fB\-r\fR \fIFILE\fR | \fB\-i\fR \fIINTERFACE\fR) [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
Decode a capture (or live interface) and print each recognized packet. See
\fBOPTIONS (decode)\fR below.
.TP
.B info \fB\-r\fR \fIFILE\fR
Print pcap file metadata and a protocol histogram. Offline files only; see
\fBLIVE CAPTURE\fR for the live equivalent.
.TP
.B interfaces
List network interfaces available for live capture. See \fBLIVE CAPTURE\fR
below.
.TP
.B policy validate (\fB\-r\fR \fIFILE\fR | \fB\-i\fR \fIINTERFACE\fR) \fB\-\-policy\fR \fIFILE\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
Zone/conduit policy check: decodes the capture (or live interface) exactly
as \fBdecode\fR would, groups it into TCP flows (for IPv4-zone conduits) and,
when the policy declares any VLAN zone, also into PROFINET RT/GOOSE/Sampled
Values/EtherCAT "Ethernet flows" keyed by protocol and MAC pair (for
VLAN-zone conduits), and checks each against the
zones/conduits declared in the policy file (a restricted, dependency-free
YAML subset -- see \fBdocs/USER_GUIDE.md\fR's POLICY FILE FORMAT section). A
zone is either a set of IPv4 CIDR blocks or a set of VLAN IDs
(\fBvlans:\fR), never both; a conduit may optionally narrow itself to
specific named functions/services
within its one protocol via \fBfunctions:\fR (e.g. Modbus reads but not
writes, IPv4-zone conduits only) -- matched, flow-wide and strict-all, against the exact
function/service name strings that protocol's decoder emits; see
\fBdocs/USER_GUIDE.md\fR's "Function-level restrictions" subsection for the
schema, the one-protocol-only rule, and the EtherNet/IP ambiguous-name
limitation. Prints a compliant/non-compliant report; see \fBOPTIONS (policy
validate)\fR and \fBEXIT STATUS\fR below.
.TP
.B inventory (\fB\-r\fR \fIFILE\fR | \fB\-i\fR \fIINTERFACE\fR) [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
Passive OT asset inventory: runs the opposite direction from \fBpolicy
validate\fR \-\- instead of checking traffic against a hand\-written policy,
infers a first\-draft zone/conduit model from a capture. Recognizes only
Modbus, DNP3, S7comm, EtherNet/IP (TCP explicit messaging and UDP/2222 CIP
I/O), and BACnet/IP traffic; builds an asset list (IP/MAC, OUI vendor
guess, protocols spoken, client\-vs\-server role) and a communication
matrix, groups assets into zones by observed subnet (\fB\-\-zone\-prefix\fR),
and infers conduits from the matrix. Can render a Mermaid or Graphviz
\fB.dot\fR diagram (\fB\-\-diagram\fR) and/or write the inferred model as a
\fBpolicy\fR\-format YAML file directly loadable by \fBpolicy validate\fR
(\fB\-\-policy\-out\fR \-\- closing the loop: discover, then enforce). See
\fBOPTIONS (inventory)\fR below and \fBdocs/USER_GUIDE.md\fR's \fBinventory\fR
section for a worked example.
.TP
.B version
Print version and build information, including whether this build has live
capture support.
.SH OPTIONS (decode)
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-read " " \fIFILE\fR
Input capture file (classic pcap or pcapng, auto-detected). Required unless \fB\-i\fR is given; mutually exclusive with it.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interface " " \fINAME\fR
Capture live from this network interface instead of reading a file. Required
unless \fB\-r\fR is given; mutually exclusive with it. Requires this build to
have libpcap/Npcap support -- see \fBLIVE CAPTURE\fR.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-filter " " \fIBPF\fR
BPF filter (tcpdump syntax). Works with both \fB\-i\fR (applied by libpcap
at capture time) and \fB\-r\fR (applied per-packet after reading the file,
same filter syntax either way); either one requires this build to have
libpcap/Npcap support -- see \fBLIVE CAPTURE\fR. \fB\-f\fR mirrors tshark's
own \fB\-f\fR.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-duration " " \fISECONDS\fR
Stop a live capture (\fB\-i\fR) after this many seconds. Default: 0
(unlimited -- rely on \fB\-\-max\-packets\fR and/or Ctrl+C instead).
\fB\-a\fR mirrors tshark's own \fB\-a\fR autostop condition, specialized
here to duration only.
.TP
.B \-\-snaplen \fIBYTES\fR
Maximum bytes captured per packet with \fB\-i\fR. Default: 65535.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-promiscuous
With \fB\-i\fR, don't put the interface into promiscuous mode. Promiscuous
is the default (see \fBLIVE CAPTURE\fR for why).
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Write output here instead of stdout. Caution: a single-dash typo of a
double-dash long option name glues onto this flag's own short form
(parsed as \fB\-o\fR followed by the rest of the typo as this flag's own
filename value) instead of erroring -- always use the double dash for a
long option name.
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-format " " {text,json,csv,fields}
Output format. Default: text. \fBfields\fR mirrors tshark's own \fB\-T
fields\fR: print only the \fB\-e\fR/\fB\-\-field\fR values requested,
tab-separated, one line per packet -- see \fB\-e\fR below and
\fBdocs/USER_GUIDE.md\fR's OUTPUT FORMATS section. \fB\-T\fR is the short
form's own letter, mirroring tshark's own \fB\-T\fR (this codebase's
\fB\-f\fR is reserved for \fB\-\-filter\fR instead, tshark's own
convention).
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-time\-format " " {e,epoch,r,relative,d,delta,a,absolute,ad,absolute\-date}
How to render each packet's timestamp. Default: r (seconds elapsed since
the first packet in this decode). Mirrors tshark's own \fB\-t\fR mnemonics
rather than tcpdump's stacking \fB\-t\fR/\fB\-tt\fR/\fB\-ttt\fR convention.
The raw-epoch rendering (\fB\-t epoch\fR) is one flag away, and the raw
epoch \fItimestamp\fR JSON field is unaffected by \fB\-t\fR regardless. See
\fBdocs/USER_GUIDE.md\fR's OUTPUT FORMATS "Timestamps" subsection.
.TP
.BR \-\-time\-offset " " {utc,local,+HH:MM,\-HH:MM}
Timezone for \fB\-\-time\-format\fR=absolute/absolute\-date; ignored by
every other \fB\-\-time\-format\fR value. Default: utc. \fBlocal\fR uses
this machine's own system timezone; a fixed offset (e.g. \fB+02:00\fR,
\fB\-0530\fR) reads a capture in the timezone of the site it came from
regardless of where it's being analyzed \-\- an extension beyond what
tshark/tcpdump themselves offer. See \fBdocs/USER_GUIDE.md\fR's OUTPUT
FORMATS "Timestamps" subsection.
.TP
.B \-\-protocol {auto,modbus,dnp3,iec104,s7comm,s7comm\-plus,mms,enip,twincat,melsec,fins,bgp,kerberos,ldap,smb,profinet,goose,sv,ethercat,stp,devicenet,bacnet,hartip,opcua,mqtt,ff\-hse,dns,mdns,llmnr,nbns,doh,rip,igmp,vrrp,hsrp,igrp,pim,eigrp,ospf,remote\-access,lateral\-movement,enterprise\-trust,eapol,wireless\-backhaul,pppoe,tunnel\-vpn,mpls,arp,lldp,slow\-protocols,winrm,dcom,ge\-srtp,bsap,cclink\-ie,codesys,coap,zigbee,cdp}
Restrict decoding to one protocol. Default: auto (tries all fourteen TCP
protocols, independent of port number, plus CIP I/O, BACnet/IP, HART-IP, and
FF-HSE on every
UDP payload,
plus PROFINET RT on every non-IPv4 Ethernet frame carrying EtherType
0x8892, plus GOOSE on every non-IPv4 Ethernet frame carrying EtherType
0x88B8, plus Sampled Values on every non-IPv4 Ethernet frame carrying
EtherType 0x88BA, plus EtherCAT on every non-IPv4 Ethernet frame carrying
EtherType 0x88A4, plus IEEE 802.1X/EAPOL on every non-IPv4 Ethernet frame
carrying EtherType 0x888E, plus PPPoE on every non-IPv4 Ethernet frame
carrying EtherType 0x8863 (Discovery) or 0x8864 (Session), plus MPLS on
every non-IPv4 Ethernet frame carrying EtherType 0x8847 (unicast) or
0x8848 (multicast), plus ARP on every non-IPv4 Ethernet frame carrying
EtherType 0x0806, plus LLDP on every non-IPv4 Ethernet frame carrying
EtherType 0x88CC, plus IEEE 802.3 Slow Protocols (LACP, Marker Protocol,
802.3 OAM/EFM \-\- told apart by a Subtype byte) on every non-IPv4 Ethernet
frame carrying EtherType 0x8809, tried right after LLDP and before STP,
plus STP/RSTP/MSTP on every classic IEEE 802.3
length-framed Ethernet frame whose LLC header is DSAP=SSAP=0x42 \-\- a
structurally separate dispatch path from every EtherType-keyed protocol
above, so there is no ordering/collision question between them; OPC UA is tried first of all, ahead of every other TCP
protocol, since its own 3-byte ASCII MessageType magic (HEL/ACK/ERR/RHE/
OPN/CLO/MSG) has zero collision risk against any other protocol's own
detection gate; EtherNet/IP is tried next, then IEC 104 before Modbus, since an
IEC 104 I-format APDU with sequence numbers 0/0 -- universal at session
start -- coincidentally also matches Modbus's MBAP-header detection,
TwinCAT/ADS (AMS/TCP, TCP port 48898) is tried right after Modbus, using
its own five-part structural gate (a Data-Length cross-check between two
independently present length fields, the ADS command State Flags bit, and
a nine-value Command ID enum -- see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's
TwinCAT / ADS section), with no collision found against any protocol
above or below it in this dispatch order,
BGP-4 (RFC 4271, TCP port 179) is tried right after TwinCAT, using the
strongest structural detection gate in this whole codebase -- a 128-bit
Marker that MUST be all-\fB0xFF\fR -- see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's
BGP-4 section -- so there is no collision risk against any protocol in
this cascade regardless of dispatch position,
MELSEC / MC Protocol (SLMP, Mitsubishi Electric, TCP port 5001, UDP port
5000) is tried right after IEC 104 but deliberately BEFORE Modbus on TCP,
using its own two-part
structural gate (an exact 3E/4E subheader magic match plus an exact
declared-length cross-check -- see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's
MELSEC / MC Protocol section); a real, post-delivery collision was found
against Modbus's own weak protocol_id==0 TCP gate (a genuine MELSEC
request with Network No.==PC No.==0 and a small I/O No. reads as a
plausible Modbus MBAP header) and fixed by this ordering, the same
"stronger gate wins" resolution IEC104-before-Modbus already uses; on UDP
it is tried right after BACnet/IP and
EtherNet/IP CIP I/O but deliberately BEFORE HART-IP, since HART-IP's own
weaker UDP gate (MessageType/MessageID both small enumerated values,
MsgLength >= 8, no magic bytes) is incidentally satisfied by real MELSEC
traffic often enough to matter \-\- discovered during this decoder's own
required manual smoke test and fixed by this ordering,
FINS (Factory Interface Network Service, Omron, TCP port 9600, UDP port
9600) is tried right after MELSEC on both transports: on TCP its own
exact 4-byte "FINS" magic is at least as strong a gate as MELSEC's own,
so it is also tried BEFORE Modbus; on UDP, which has no magic bytes at
all, its own multi-field gate (ICF reserved bits zero, RSV exactly zero,
and an exact match against a 17-command allowlist) is tried BEFORE
HART-IP for the same reason MELSEC is \-\- HART-IP's own weak gate has a
real, partial overlap with FINS's own RSV/GCT byte layout too, confirmed
against a real FINS/TCP probe's own observed field values \-\- see
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's FINS section for the full collision
surveys,
Kerberos (RFC 4120, TCP and UDP port 88) is tried right after TwinCAT on
TCP and right after HART-IP on UDP, using its outer ASN.1 APPLICATION tag
byte (one of 7 fixed values) plus, on full decode, a pvno/msg-type
cross-check -- see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's Kerberos section --
with no collision found against any protocol tried on either transport,
LDAP (RFC 4511, TCP port 389/3268, no UDP sibling) is tried right after
Kerberos on TCP, using the same three-part structural check
\fB\-\-protocol enterprise\-trust\fR's own LDAP recognition already
established (outer SEQUENCE tag, INTEGER messageID, a valid protocolOp
APPLICATION tag) plus, on full decode, a per-op constructed-bit check
this decoder adds -- a real tag-byte collision against five of Kerberos's
own message types was found and resolved during planning (RFC 4511 uses
IMPLICIT tagging, so LDAP's per-operation tags only ever appear several
bytes inside a message, never as its leading byte, so it does not
actually collide with Kerberos's own gate, which reads the leading byte)
-- see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's LDAP section for the full
collision survey,
SMB2/NTLM (MS-SMB2/MS-NLMP, TCP port 445/139, no UDP sibling) is tried
right after LDAP on TCP, using the same 4-byte \fB0xFF\fR/\fB0xFE\fR/
\fB0xFD\fR + "SMB" magic \fB\-\-protocol lateral\-movement\fR's own SMB
recognition already established, now additionally requiring the 4-byte
Zero+StreamProtocolLength Direct-TCP/NBSS prefix on both ports -- already
proven collision-free in this cascade before this decoder existed, so no
new collision survey was needed -- see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's
SMB2/NTLM section for the full writeup,
Netlogon/DCE-RPC (MS-NRPC/MS-RPCE) has no dispatch-order entry or wire
gate of its own at all -- it is recognized purely structurally, via a
SMB2 \fBFileId\fR already opened against a pipe named \fBnetlogon\fR, so
it adds nothing to this collision survey -- see
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's Netlogon/DCE-RPC section for the full
writeup,
SAMR/LSARPC (MS-SAMR, MS-LSAD/MS-LSAT) likewise have no dispatch-order
entry or wire gate of their own -- recognized structurally, via a SMB2
\fBFileId\fR already opened against a pipe named \fBsamr\fR or
\fBlsarpc\fR, so they too add nothing to this collision survey -- see
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's SAMR + LSARPC section for the full
writeup,
SRVSVC/WKSSVC (MS-SRVS, MS-WKST) likewise have no dispatch-order entry or
wire gate of their own -- recognized structurally, via a SMB2 \fBFileId\fR
already opened against a pipe named \fBsrvsvc\fR or \fBwkssvc\fR, so they
too add nothing to this collision survey -- see
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's SRVSVC + WKSSVC section for the full
writeup,
DRSUAPI (MS-DRSR) likewise has no dispatch-order entry or wire gate of
its own -- recognized structurally, via a SMB2 \fBFileId\fR already
opened against a pipe named \fBdrsuapi\fR (this codebase's own synthetic
gate name; see the caveat above), so it too adds nothing to this
collision survey -- see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's DRSUAPI section
for the full writeup, including its own transport scope caveat,
WinRM (MS-WSMV) IS wire-gated (\fBGateKind::TcpPort\fR, port 5985, like
DoH), and it_protocols.cpp's own generic Tier-2 "http" recognition
already claims this same HTTP/1.1 framing name-only, port-independently
\-\- a genuine collision, resolved by wiring WinRM's own decoder ahead of
that generic check in decoder.cpp's dispatch chain (a pinning test proves
it), the same resolution this collision survey's own RDP-vs-COTP and
FTP/LDAP-vs-MQTT entries below already use; a second, unrelated,
pre-existing collision (MQTT's own port-independent structural check
false-matching a WinRM \fBPOST\fR request's own leading bytes on a
non-standard port) was found and documented, not fixed, since it is not
this decoder's own collision to fix \-\- see
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's WinRM section for the full writeup,
DCOM (MS-DCOM) IS wire-gated too (\fBGateKind::TcpPort\fR, port 135, like
DoH/WinRM), but unlike WinRM there was no pre-existing recognizer for it
to collide with at all \-\- port 135 had zero prior recognition anywhere
in this codebase (confirmed by grep before this decoder was written), so
it stays port-gated in Auto mode purely because its own structural gate
(\fBrpc_vers == 5\fR plus a plausible \fBfrag_length\fR) is weaker than
SMB's own magic-byte check, not to resolve any actual collision \-\- see
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's DCOM section for the full writeup,
S7comm\-Plus is tried on the same TPKT/COTP framing and TCP port 102 as
S7comm and MMS, disambiguated by its own protocol id byte (0x72 vs S7comm's
0x32, so no collision risk with either), and MMS is
tried immediately after (same TPKT/COTP framing, same TCP port
102, S7comm's own stronger single-byte protocol-id gate tried first) using
its own separate structural gate (a plausible Session SPDU type, a "bare
MMS" PDU tag, or "bare Presentation" bytes -- see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's MMS section), and
HART-IP is tried third-to-last, on both TCP and UDP, since its own 8-byte
header check was the weakest structural gate of any protocol here and is
known to collide with Modbus/TCP's own detection on TCP (never resolved by
reordering -- see
\fBdocs/DEVELOPMENT.md\fR's PROTOCOL DETECTION section), MQTT is tried
second-to-last: it has no fixed magic number or length field of its own, so
it only gets a turn once every other protocol except FF-HSE (HART-IP
included) has already
declined the bytes; real validation nonetheless found two genuine
collisions this ordering alone didn't resolve -- a v5 CONNACK payload that
coincidentally satisfied Modbus/TCP's own \fIprotocol_id==0\fR tell (fixed
with a length-plausibility cap in Modbus's TCP parser) and small MQTT
packet identifiers that coincidentally satisfied HART-IP's own weak gate
(worked around on the test-fixture side, deliberately without weakening
HART-IP's own gate) -- see \fBdocs/DEVELOPMENT.md\fR's PROTOCOL DETECTION
section, and FF-HSE is tried dead LAST of all, on both TCP and UDP: its own
structural gate is a SINGLE byte at header offset 2 landing on one of 12
valid values out of 256, honestly the weakest gate in this whole codebase,
weaker even than MQTT's own -- see \fBdocs/USER_GUIDE.md\fR's PROTOCOL
DETECTION section's "Why FF-HSE is tried last of all". \fBenip\fR covers both
EtherNet/IP explicit messaging (TCP) and CIP I/O implicit messaging (UDP).
\fBmms\fR is IEC 61850 MMS -- shares S7comm's exact transport/port but is a
distinct protocol; there is no separate \fB\-\-mms\-port\fR, see
\fB\-\-s7comm\-port\fR below.
\fBsv\fR is IEC 61850-9-2 Sampled Values. \fBethercat\fR is EtherCAT.
\fBstp\fR is Spanning Tree Protocol (STP/RSTP/MSTP) \-\- no port option,
since it rides classic 802.3/LLC framing with no port at all.
\fBdevicenet\fR is DeviceNet (CAN-bus CIP) \-\- no port option either, and
meaningful only against a \fILINKTYPE_CAN_SOCKETCAN\fR capture (gated on
the capture's own pcap link type, checked before any protocol filter, so
it has no effect on an ordinary Ethernet-linktype capture).
\fBbacnet\fR is BACnet/IP.
\fBhartip\fR is HART-IP, tried on both TCP and UDP.
\fBff\-hse\fR is FOUNDATION Fieldbus HSE (FDA/SM/FMS/LAN Redundancy), tried
on both TCP and UDP.
\fBprofinet\fR covers both DCP and cyclic real-time IO.
\fBopcua\fR is OPC UA Binary (UA-TCP/Secure Conversation), TCP only.
\fBdns\fR/\fBmdns\fR/\fBllmnr\fR (UDP ports 53/5353/5355) and \fBnbns\fR
(NetBIOS Name Service, UDP port 137) share one wire format family (DNS/
mDNS/LLMNR) or a closely related one (NBT-NS), but are the only protocols
in this tool that are port-GATED in \fBauto\fR mode rather than tried
opportunistically port-independent \-\- none of the four has a
self-describing wire-format signal strong enough to check safely against
every UDP payload the way every other protocol here does. Selecting one of
these four explicitly skips the port gate entirely, the same opportunistic
posture every other protocol gets by default. \fBdoh\fR (DNS-over-HTTPS
detection, TCP port 443) is detection-only \-\- its actual DNS content is
TLS-encrypted and never decoded \-\- and works by matching a TLS
ClientHello's plaintext SNI against a curated table of known public DoH
resolvers; it is likewise port-gated in \fBauto\fR mode.
\fBrip\fR (UDP port 520) and \fBhsrp\fR (UDP port 1985) join that same
port-gated group, for the identical reason \-\- neither has a
self-describing wire-format signal strong enough to check safely against
every UDP payload. Unlike dns/mdns/llmnr/nbns, though, rip/hsrp are tried
BEFORE FF-HSE's own opportunistic check rather than after it: a synthetic
HSRPv1 message was found, while building this feature, to satisfy FF-HSE's
own weaker structural gate and get misdetected as truncated FF-HSE traffic
in the other order.
\fBigmp\fR (IP protocol 2), \fBvrrp\fR (IP protocol 112), \fBigrp\fR (IP
protocol 9), \fBpim\fR (IP protocol 103), \fBeigrp\fR (IP protocol 88), and
\fBospf\fR (IP protocol 89) need no port gate or option at all \-\- all six
ride directly on IP with no UDP/TCP header, dispatched purely by their own
IANA-exclusive IP protocol number. \fBigrp\fR, \fBpim\fR, \fBeigrp\fR, and
\fBospf\fR are a follow-up batch alongside rip/igmp/vrrp/hsrp above; BGP
(which rides over TCP port 179 instead) is deliberately not yet covered and
planned for a later round \-\- see \fBdocs/DEVELOPMENT.md\fR's ROADMAP section.
\fBtunnel\-vpn\fR covers the fourteen port/IP-protocol-number-based
protocols of Tier 5 of the "IT protocols an OT auditor flags" family (GRE/
NVGRE/EoIP, ESP, AH, IP-in-IP, 6in4, L2TP, IKE, VXLAN, Geneve, WireGuard,
OpenVPN, dtls\-tunnel, STT \-\- each its own \fBprotocol\fR value under this
one filter name); see \fB\-\-tunnel\-vpn\-port\fR below and
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's "Tier 5 generic tunnel/VPN
encapsulation recognition" section. \fBmpls\fR is Tier 5's sixteenth
protocol, MPLS \-\- EtherType-keyed, no port at all, the same split
\fBeapol\fR/\fBpppoe\fR have from \fBenterprise\-trust\fR/
\fBwireless\-backhaul\fR; no port option exists for it either.
See \fBdocs/DEVELOPMENT.md\fR's PROTOCOL DETECTION section and
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR for the full detail.
.TP
.B \-\-modbus\-port \fIPORT\fR
Additional TCP port to treat as expected for Modbus. Repeatable. Does not
gate detection, only whether the port is flagged as unexpected in the output.
.TP
.B \-\-dnp3\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-modbus\-port\fR, for DNP3. Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-iec104\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-modbus\-port\fR, for IEC 60870-5-104. Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-s7comm\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-modbus\-port\fR, for COTP/S7comm, MMS, and S7comm\-Plus
(repeatable) \-\- all three ride the identical TPKT/COTP transport and TCP
port 102, and there is no separate \fB\-\-mms\-port\fR or
\fB\-\-s7comm\-plus\-port\fR. Does not change detection, only whether the
port is flagged as unexpected.
.TP
.B \-\-enip\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-modbus\-port\fR, for EtherNet/IP explicit messaging (TCP). Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-enip\-io\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-modbus\-port\fR, for EtherNet/IP CIP I/O implicit messaging (UDP). Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-bacnet\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-modbus\-port\fR, for BACnet/IP (UDP). Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-hartip\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-modbus\-port\fR, for HART-IP. Repeatable. Applies to both TCP
and UDP, since HART-IP uses the same port number on either transport.
.TP
.B \-\-kerberos\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-modbus\-port\fR, for Kerberos (built\-in default: 88).
Repeatable. Applies to both TCP and UDP, since Kerberos uses the same port
number on either transport.
.TP
.B \-\-ldap\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-modbus\-port\fR, for LDAP (built\-in default: 389 and 3268).
Repeatable. TCP only \-\- LDAP has no UDP sibling in this decoder. Does not
change detection (LDAP is recognized on any TCP port), only whether the
port is flagged as unexpected.
.TP
.B \-\-smb\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-modbus\-port\fR, for SMB (built\-in default: 445 and 139).
Repeatable. TCP only \-\- SMB has no UDP sibling in this decoder. Does not
change detection (SMB is recognized on any TCP port), only whether the
port is flagged as unexpected.
.TP
.B \-\-opcua\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-modbus\-port\fR, for OPC UA. Repeatable. TCP only.
.TP
.B \-\-mqtt\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-modbus\-port\fR, for MQTT (built\-in default: 1883).
Repeatable. TCP only.
.TP
.B \-\-bgp\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-modbus\-port\fR, for BGP (built\-in default: 179).
Repeatable. TCP only.
.TP
.B \-\-ffhse\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-modbus\-port\fR, for FOUNDATION Fieldbus HSE (built\-in
defaults: 1089/1090/1091/3622). Repeatable. Applies to both TCP and UDP,
and shared across FDA/SM/FMS/LAN Redundancy \-\- the sub-protocol is
signaled in-band by the header, not by port.
.TP
.B \-\-dns\-port \fIPORT\fR
Additional UDP port to treat as expected for DNS (built\-in default: 53).
Repeatable. \fBUNLIKE\fR every \fB\-\-*\-port\fR option above, this DOES
widen detection in \fBauto\fR mode, not just whether the port is flagged as
unexpected \-\- see \fB\-\-protocol\fR above and \fBdocs/DEVELOPMENT.md\fR's
PROTOCOL DETECTION section for why DNS/mDNS/LLMNR/NBT-NS/DoH are
port-gated in the first place.
.TP
.B \-\-mdns\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-dns\-port\fR, for mDNS (built\-in default: 5353). Repeatable.
Also widens detection in \fBauto\fR mode.
.TP
.B \-\-llmnr\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-dns\-port\fR, for LLMNR (built\-in default: 5355). Repeatable.
Also widens detection in \fBauto\fR mode.
.TP
.B \-\-nbns\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-dns\-port\fR, for NetBIOS Name Service/NBT-NS (built\-in
default: 137). Repeatable. Also widens detection in \fBauto\fR mode.
.TP
.B \-\-doh\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-dns\-port\fR, for DNS-over-HTTPS detection (built\-in
default: 443, TCP). Repeatable. Also widens detection in \fBauto\fR mode.
.TP
.B \-\-winrm\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-dns\-port\fR, for WS-Management/WinRM (built\-in default:
5985, TCP, plaintext only \-\- port 5986/TLS is out of scope). Repeatable.
Also widens detection in \fBauto\fR mode.
.TP
.B \-\-dcom\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-dns\-port\fR, for DCOM activation (built\-in default: 135,
TCP \-\- structural activation/OXID-resolution recognition only, see
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's DCOM section). Repeatable. Also widens
detection in \fBauto\fR mode.
.TP
.B \-\-ge\-srtp\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-dns\-port\fR, for GE SRTP (built\-in default: 18245, TCP \-\-
the secondary port 18246 documented by GE is NOT auto\-detected and must be
named here, or reached via \fB\-\-protocol ge\-srtp\fR, which tries GE SRTP
port\-independently; see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's GE SRTP
section). Repeatable. Also widens detection in \fBauto\fR mode.
.TP
.B \-\-bsap\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-dns\-port\fR, for BSAP (built\-in default: 1234, UDP \-\-
structural link\-layer decode only, no RDB application\-layer semantics;
see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's BSAP section). Repeatable. Also
widens detection in \fBauto\fR mode.
.TP
.B \-\-cclink\-ie\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-dns\-port\fR, for CC\-Link IE Field Network Basic (built\-in
defaults: 61450 cyclic data, 61451 SLMP node search/Set IP Address; see
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's CC\-Link IE section). Repeatable. Unlike
\fB\-\-bsap\-port\fR, this does NOT gate detection \-\- CC\-Link IE is
already tried opportunistically on every UDP port in \fBauto\fR mode (its
own command\-code gate is what prevents false positives); this option only
widens the ports annotated with a "non\-standard port" note when traffic
is recognized on a port other than the two built\-in defaults.
.TP
.B \-\-codesys\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-dns\-port\fR, for CODESYS V3 (built\-in defaults: 11740/1217
TCP, Block Driver\-framed; 1740\-1743 UDP, unframed; see
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's CODESYS section). Repeatable. Unlike
most \fB\-\-*\-port\fR options, this does NOT gate detection \-\- CODESYS is
already tried opportunistically on every TCP and UDP port in \fBauto\fR
mode (its own magic\-number/length gates are what prevent false
positives), the same posture as \fB\-\-cclink\-ie\-port\fR; this option only
widens the ports annotated as expected rather than flagged as
non\-standard.
.TP
.B \-\-coap\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-dns\-port\fR, for CoAP (Constrained Application Protocol,
RFC 7252; built\-in default: 5683, UDP; see
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's CoAP section). Repeatable. UNLIKE
\fB\-\-codesys\-port\fR/\fB\-\-cclink\-ie\-port\fR above, this DOES gate
detection \-\- CoAP is only tried on a configured/expected port in
\fBauto\fR mode, the same posture as \fB\-\-bsap\-port\fR/\fB\-\-rip\-port\fR/
\fB\-\-hsrp\-port\fR (its own shortest legal message is too weak a
structural signal to try opportunistically on every UDP port).
.TP
.B \-\-flood\-threshold \fIN\fR
Per\-destination packet count (summed across the whole capture, not a
real packets\-per\-second rate) at which the SYN\-flood, ACK\-flood,
ICMP\-flood, UDP\-flood, and generic TCP\-flood catch\-all attack\-detection
signatures each fire their own note, once per destination. Default: 100,
an explicitly documented judgment call empirically set clear of this
project's own busiest legitimate fixture, not a vendor\-sourced number;
see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's Attack Detection section. A
broadcast\-looking destination address is never counted toward any flood
threshold, since a flood by definition targets one specific host.
.TP
.B \-\-rip\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-dns\-port\fR, for RIP (built\-in default: 520). Repeatable.
Also widens detection in \fBauto\fR mode.
.TP
.B \-\-hsrp\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-dns\-port\fR, for HSRP (built\-in default: 1985). Repeatable.
Also widens detection in \fBauto\fR mode. IGMP and VRRP need no port option
at all \-\- both are dispatched purely by IP protocol number.
.TP
.B \-\-remote\-access\-port \fIPORT\fR
Additional TCP or UDP port to treat as expected for the Tier 1 "IT
protocols an OT auditor flags" family (RDP/VNC/TeamViewer/AnyDesk/Zoom;
built\-in defaults: 3389, 5900\-5906, 5938, 7070, 8801\-8810, 3478\-3479).
Repeatable. One shared option across all five. Widens detection in
\fBauto\fR mode for RDP's COTP\-gated check and the three port\-only
protocols (TeamViewer/AnyDesk/Zoom) \-\- VNC's own RFB banner check is
never port\-gated regardless.
.TP
.B \-\-lateral\-movement\-port \fIPORT\fR
Additional TCP or UDP port to treat as expected for the Tier 2 "IT
protocols an OT auditor flags" family (SSH/HTTP/HTTPS/SNMPv1v2c/
Telnet/FTP/TFTP \-\- SMB was promoted to its own dedicated decoder, see
\fB\-\-smb\-port\fR above; built\-in defaults: 22, 80/8080/8000, 443/8443,
161, 162, 23, 21, 69).
Repeatable. One shared option across all seven. Widens detection in
\fBauto\fR mode for SNMP/Telnet/FTP/
TFTP's own port\-gated checks and HTTPS's port\-only fallback \-\- SSH's
version\-exchange banner and HTTP's request\-line/status\-line are never
port\-gated regardless.
.TP
.B \-\-enterprise\-trust\-port \fIPORT\fR
Additional TCP or UDP port to treat as expected for the Tier 3 "IT
protocols an OT auditor flags" family (NTP/DHCP/LDAP/LDAPS/RADIUS/
TACACS+; built\-in defaults: 123, 67/68, 389/3268, 636/3269, 1812/1813/
1645/1646, 49). Repeatable. One shared option across all six. Widens
detection in \fBauto\fR mode for NTP/LDAP/RADIUS/TACACS+'s own port\-gated
checks and LDAPS's port\-only fallback \-\- DHCP's magic cookie is never
port\-gated regardless. IEEE 802.1X/EAPOL needs no port option at all \-\-
it has no port, being EtherType\-keyed (0x888E) like PROFINET/GOOSE/SV/
EtherCAT.
.TP
.B \-\-wireless\-backhaul\-port \fIPORT\fR
Additional UDP port to treat as expected for the Tier 4 "IT protocols an
OT auditor flags" family (CAPWAP control/data, LWAPP control/data, GTP\-U;
built\-in defaults: 5246, 5247, 12222, 12223, 2152). Repeatable. One
shared option across all five. Unlike Tier 3, widens detection in
\fBauto\fR mode for all five, since none of this tier's own structural
checks are strong enough to run port\-independently. PPPoE needs no port
option at all \-\- it has no port, being EtherType\-keyed (0x8863/0x8864)
like EAPOL.
.TP
.B \-\-tunnel\-vpn\-port \fIPORT\fR
Additional TCP or UDP port to treat as expected for the Tier 5 "IT
protocols an OT auditor flags" family (IKE, L2TP, VXLAN, Geneve,
WireGuard, OpenVPN; built\-in defaults: 500/4500, 1701, 4789, 6081,
51820, 1194/1194). Repeatable. One shared option across all six
port-gated protocols. Widens detection in \fBauto\fR mode for all six,
the same "always gates detection itself" treatment
\fB\-\-wireless\-backhaul\-port\fR gets for Tier 4. GRE, ESP, AH,
IP-in-IP, 6in4, and L2TPv3's own direct-IP form need no port option at
all \-\- they ride raw IP, dispatched purely by IP protocol number, the
same no-port precedent IGMP/VRRP/IGRP/PIM/EIGRP/OSPF already established.
The generic dtls\-tunnel check is never port\-gated and is unaffected by
this option. MPLS needs no port option either \-\- it has no port, being
EtherType\-keyed (0x8847/0x8848) like EAPOL/PPPoE.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-max\-packets " " \fIN\fR
Stop after decoding this many packets. Default: 0 (unlimited). With
\fB\-i\fR, also bounds a live capture. \fB\-c\fR mirrors tshark's own
\fB\-c\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-reassembly\-bytes \fIN\fR
Override every cross-segment payload-buffering byte cap at once: the
general TCP reassembly path (default 16 MiB), DNP3 fragment reassembly
(default 64 KiB), COTP TSDU reassembly (default 1 MiB), and OPC UA's/
FF-HSE's own declared-length plausibility ceiling (default 16 MiB each).
Default: 0 (leave every site at its own default). See
\fBdocs/DEVELOPMENT.md\fR's "External code review and engineering
priorities" item 7 for the full constant-by-constant mapping.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-reassembly\-segments \fIN\fR
Override every cross-segment frame/segment-count cap at once: the general
TCP reassembly path (default 20,000 segments), DNP3 fragment reassembly
(default 500 frames), and COTP TSDU reassembly (default 2,000 frames).
Default: 0 (leave every site at its own default).
.TP
.B \-\-max\-recursion\-depth \fIN\fR
Override every recursive-decode depth cap at once: MMS Data-value nesting
(default 32), EtherNet/IP CIP Multiple_Service_Packet/Unconnected_Send
nesting (default 4), MPLS label-stack depth (default 16), S7comm-Plus
struct/item nesting (default 16), GOOSE Data ASN.1 nesting (default 6),
and DNS name-compression pointer-hop guard (default 128). Default: 0
(leave every site at its own default).
.TP
.B \-\-max\-decoded\-objects \fIN\fR
Override every per-message decoded-object/value/list-entry cap at once
(dozens of individually-named constants across DNP3/IEC104/GOOSE/
EtherNet-IP/S7comm-Plus/MQTT/PROFINET/EtherCAT/Sampled Values/decoder.cpp's
own summary lists, plus the 9 duplicated 50-entry list caps shared by
EIGRP/OSPF/PIM/IGMP/ICMP/IGRP/RIP/VRRP/HSRP). Default: 0 (leave every site
at its own default); too many constants to enumerate here \-\- see
\fBdocs/DEVELOPMENT.md\fR item 7 for the full mapping.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-coalesced\-messages \fIN\fR
Override every "N application-layer messages found coalesced in one TCP/
UDP payload" cap at once: FF-HSE, HART-IP, MQTT, EtherNet/IP, OPC UA,
DNP3, and IEC 104 (all default 50). Default: 0 (leave every site at its
own default).
.TP
.B \-\-max\-active\-flows \fIN\fR
Cap the number of distinct TCP flows the general cross-segment reassembly
path tracks state for at once, regardless of how many distinct flows the
capture contains \-\- an existing flow's own state being updated never
counts against this. Default: 0 (unbounded). A different category from
the five flags above: those bound the cost of any ONE flow/reassembly/
message; this bounds the COUNT of distinct flows tracked at once. See
\fBdocs/DEVELOPMENT.md\fR's security review write-up (finding 1) for the
full rationale.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-flow\-state\-entries \fIN\fR
Cap the total number of distinct sessions/flows tracked at once across
every protocol's own state (SMB pipes, DCE/RPC interfaces, Kerberos,
LDAP, WinRM, DCOM, Modbus/TwinCAT/MELSEC/MQTT, DNP3/COTP reassembly, and
more), combined. Default: 0 (unbounded). The same category of fix as
\fB\-\-max\-active\-flows\fR, for the registration-model decoders' own
per-session state instead of the general TCP reassembly path.
.TP
.B \-\-stats
Print an aggregate summary instead of one line per packet. Ignores
\fB\-\-format\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-strict
Abort on the first malformed packet instead of warning and continuing.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Show per-packet notes (longer-form contextual/security observations) and
the trailing "(client X \-\- tier)" direction-source suffix; both are
suppressed by default to keep default output readable, since on a busy
capture the notes in particular can swamp the per-packet lines. Off by
default. Text output only (\fB\-T text\fR, the default) \-\- JSON/CSV
always include notes/direction fields unconditionally, same as every
other field. \fB\-\-no\-direction\fR still suppresses the direction
suffix even under \fB\-v\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-redact ", " \-\-no\-redact
Mask cleartext authentication secrets found while decoding (HSRP/VRRP
authentication data, OPC UA \fBActivateSessionRequest\fR passwords, MQTT
\fBCONNECT\fR passwords) with \fB[REDACTED]\fR wherever they would
otherwise appear \-\- summary/notes text and JSON value fields alike \-\-
so output can be shared safely by default. On by default;
\fB\-\-no\-redact\fR shows the real cleartext values (useful for local
triage/incident response where the analyst is already trusted with the
capture itself). Usernames are never redacted, only passwords/
authentication data.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-vlan
Disable display of the 802.1Q VLAN ID for a VLAN-tagged packet. On by
default. See \fBdocs/USER_GUIDE.md\fR's OUTPUT FORMATS section.
.TP
.B \-\-ether
For a packet with an IP layer, show its Ethernet header (source/destination
MAC address, VLAN tag) below the packet line in text output \-\- mirrors
tcpdump's own \fB\-e\fR (long-form only in this codebase \-\- \fB\-e\fR
itself is reserved for \fB\-\-field\fR below, tshark's own convention). Off
by default, to keep output compact; implied by \fB\-\-mac\-vendor\fR
(there'd be nothing to attach a vendor name to otherwise). A no-op for a
packet with no IP layer at all (ARP/LLDP/EAPOL/PPPoE/MPLS/
PROFINET RT/GOOSE/Sampled Values/EtherCAT/STP/etc.), since that packet's
MAC address pair is already shown directly on its own head line
unconditionally, regardless of this flag. Only affects text output \-\-
JSON/CSV always include \fBsrc_mac\fR/\fBdst_mac\fR as base fields, same as
\fBsrc_ip\fR/\fBdst_ip\fR. See \fBdocs/USER_GUIDE.md\fR's OUTPUT FORMATS
"Name resolution" subsection.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-field " " \fIFIELD\fR
With \fB\-T fields\fR, print this field's value (repeatable, printed in the
order given, tab-separated). Mirrors tshark's own \fB\-e\fR. The field name
is whatever key appears in this tool's own \fB\-\-format json\fR output for
that packet (e.g. \fBsrc_ip\fR, \fBdst_port\fR, \fBmodbus_function_code\fR);
a field absent for a given packet (wrong protocol, or an optional field not
present) prints as an empty column rather than an error. Requires
\fB\-T fields\fR \-\- see \fB\-T\fR/\fB\-\-format\fR above.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-write " " \fIFILE\fR
Write every packet that reaches this run (after \fB\-f\fR/\fB\-\-filter\fR,
if given) to this path as a new classic-pcap capture file, raw and
unmodified \-\- mirrors tshark/tcpdump's own \fB\-w\fR. Works identically
whether packets come from a live capture (\fB\-i\fR) or an offline read
(\fB\-r\fR); does not change or replace the normal \fB\-\-format\fR output,
which continues to stdout/\fB\-o\fR exactly as without \fB\-w\fR.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-hex
Print a hex+ASCII dump of each packet's raw bytes below its normal decode
line \-\- mirrors tshark's own \fB\-x\fR. Text output only
(\fB\-T text\fR, the default); ignored under \fB\-T json\fR/\fBcsv\fR/
\fBfields\fR and under \fB\-\-stats\fR, which have no per-packet line to
attach it to.
.TP
.B \-\-mac\-vendor
Enable OUI (MAC vendor) resolution against the built-in table (IEEE MA-L/
MA-M/MA-S registry data via nmap's nmap-mac-prefixes redistribution, see
\fBtools/generate_oui_table.py\fR). Off by default, to keep output compact.
Implies \fB\-\-ether\fR. Named
.B \-\-mac\-vendor
rather than
.B \-\-oui
specifically so a single-dash typo of this flag reports a clean error
instead of silently gluing onto \fB\-o\fR/\fB\-\-output\fR the way a
single-dash \fB\-oui\fR used to.
See \fBdocs/USER_GUIDE.md\fR's OUTPUT FORMATS "Name resolution" subsection.
.TP
.B \-\-resolve
Enable hostname resolution from an explicitly-supplied \fB\-\-hosts\fR file.
Off by default. File-only \-\- \fBnever\fR performs live DNS, under any flag
combination. Given with no \fB\-\-hosts\fR, this is a harmless no-op (a
one-line advisory note is printed, not an error).
.TP
.B \-\-hosts \fIFILE\fR
Unix /etc/hosts-style file to resolve IP addresses from, for
\fB\-\-resolve\fR. Must exist.
.TP
.B \-\-nn
Disable service name (port\->name) resolution, from the built-in table and
\fB\-\-services\fR alike. On by default. Named after the
\fBnc\fR/\fBnmap\fR/\fBtcpdump\fR-family \fB\-n\fR/\fB\-nn\fR convention.
.TP
.B \-\-services \fIFILE\fR
Unix /etc/services-style file to supplement/override the built-in
port\->service-name table. Must exist.
.SH OPTIONS (policy validate)
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-read " " \fIFILE\fR
Input capture file (classic pcap or pcapng, auto-detected). Required unless \fB\-i\fR is given; mutually exclusive with it.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interface " " \fINAME\fR
Check live traffic from this network interface instead of reading a file.
Required unless \fB\-r\fR is given; mutually exclusive with it. No
\fB\-\-max\-packets\fR equivalent here -- use \fB\-\-duration\fR and/or Ctrl+C.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-filter " " \fIBPF\fR
BPF filter (tcpdump syntax). Works with both \fB\-i\fR and \fB\-r\fR; see
\fBdecode\fR's own \fB\-f\fR/\fB\-\-filter\fR above.
.TP
.B \-\-duration \fISECONDS\fR
Stop a live capture (\fB\-i\fR) after this many seconds. Default: 0
(unlimited -- rely on Ctrl+C instead).
.TP
.B \-\-snaplen \fIBYTES\fR
Maximum bytes captured per packet with \fB\-i\fR. Default: 65535.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-promiscuous
Same meaning as \fBdecode\fR's \fB\-\-no\-promiscuous\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-policy \fIFILE\fR
Zone/conduit policy file. Required. See \fBdocs/USER_GUIDE.md\fR's POLICY FILE
FORMAT section for the schema, including the optional per-conduit
\fBfunctions:\fR (singular alias \fBfunction:\fR) allow-list restricting a
conduit to specific named functions/services within its one protocol.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Write the report here instead of stdout. Caution: a single-dash typo of a
double-dash long option name glues onto this flag's own short form instead
of erroring -- always use the double dash for a long option name.
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-format " " {text,json}
Report format. Default: text.
.TP
.B \-\-strict
Abort on the first malformed packet instead of warning and continuing.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-reassembly\-bytes \fIN\fR ", " \-\-max\-reassembly\-segments \fIN\fR ", " \-\-max\-recursion\-depth \fIN\fR ", " \-\-max\-decoded\-objects \fIN\fR ", " \-\-max\-coalesced\-messages \fIN\fR
Same five resource-exhaustion-limit overrides as \fBdecode\fR's own flags
of the same name above (see their full description there); apply
identically here since this command decodes the same traffic. Each
defaults to 0 (leave every site at its own compile-time default).
.TP
.B \-\-strict\-it\-protocols
Also fail compliance (non\-zero exit code) when the report's own "notable
protocols" finding (see \fBdocs/USER_GUIDE.md\fR's POLICY FILE FORMAT
"Notable IT protocols" subsection) is non\-empty, even on an otherwise
COMPLIANT capture. Off by default \-\- the finding is always reported
regardless of this flag; it only controls whether it additionally affects
the exit code. Never affects the report's own COMPLIANT/NON\-COMPLIANT text
or JSON \fBcompliant\fR field.
.TP
.B \-\-summarize\-unclassified
Collapse UNCLASSIFIED TRAFFIC (and, if present, ETHERNET UNCLASSIFIED
TRAFFIC) entries that share the same endpoints/port/protocol(s)/zones into
one summary line carrying a flow count and total packet count, instead of
one full block per individual flow. Off by default. Aimed at a busy
capture with far more distinct TCP flows (a new source port on every
reconnect) than distinct (client, server, port) patterns actually worth
reviewing. VIOLATIONS/ALLOWED (and their Ethernet equivalents) are never
summarized, only the unclassified groups. Only affects \fB\-\-format
text\fR \-\- JSON always lists every flow individually regardless of this
flag.
.TP
.B \-\-mac\-vendor
Same meaning as \fBdecode\fR's \fB\-\-mac\-vendor\fR: enable OUI (MAC vendor)
resolution against the built-in table, applied to the report's flow MAC
addresses. Off by default. See \fBdocs/USER_GUIDE.md\fR's OUTPUT FORMATS "Name
resolution" subsection.
.TP
.B \-\-resolve
Same meaning as \fBdecode\fR's \fB\-\-resolve\fR: enable hostname resolution
from an explicitly-supplied \fB\-\-hosts\fR file, applied to the report's
flow IP addresses. Off by default. File-only \-\- \fBnever\fR performs live
DNS, under any flag combination.
.TP
.B \-\-hosts \fIFILE\fR
Unix /etc/hosts-style file to resolve IP addresses from, for
\fB\-\-resolve\fR. Must exist.
.TP
.B \-\-nn
Same meaning as \fBdecode\fR's \fB\-\-nn\fR: disable service name
(port\->name) resolution, applied to the report's flow server port. On by
default.
.TP
.B \-\-services \fIFILE\fR
Unix /etc/services-style file to supplement/override the built-in
port\->service-name table. Must exist.
.SH OPTIONS (inventory)
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-read " " \fIFILE\fR
Input capture file (classic pcap or pcapng, auto-detected). Required unless \fB\-i\fR is given; mutually exclusive with it.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interface " " \fINAME\fR
Build the inventory from live traffic on this network interface instead of
reading a file. Required unless \fB\-r\fR is given; mutually exclusive with it.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-filter " " \fIBPF\fR
BPF filter (tcpdump syntax). Works with both \fB\-i\fR and \fB\-r\fR; see
\fBdecode\fR's own \fB\-f\fR/\fB\-\-filter\fR above.
.TP
.B \-\-duration \fISECONDS\fR
Stop a live capture (\fB\-i\fR) after this many seconds. Default: 0
(unlimited -- rely on Ctrl+C instead).
.TP
.B \-\-snaplen \fIBYTES\fR
Maximum bytes captured per packet with \fB\-i\fR. Default: 65535.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-promiscuous
Same meaning as \fBdecode\fR's \fB\-\-no\-promiscuous\fR.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Write the report here instead of stdout. Caution: a single-dash typo of a
double-dash long option name glues onto this flag's own short form instead
of erroring -- always use the double dash for a long option name.
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-format " " {text,json}
Report format. Default: text.
.TP
.B \-\-strict
Abort on the first malformed packet instead of warning and continuing.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-reassembly\-bytes \fIN\fR ", " \-\-max\-reassembly\-segments \fIN\fR ", " \-\-max\-recursion\-depth \fIN\fR ", " \-\-max\-decoded\-objects \fIN\fR ", " \-\-max\-coalesced\-messages \fIN\fR
Same five resource-exhaustion-limit overrides as \fBdecode\fR's own flags
of the same name above (see their full description there); apply
identically here since this command decodes the same traffic. Each
defaults to 0 (leave every site at its own compile-time default).
.TP
.B \-\-zone\-prefix \fIN\fR
CIDR prefix length (0\-32) inferred zones are grouped by: every observed
asset IP is masked to this many bits, one zone per distinct resulting
network. Default: 24.
.TP
.B \-\-diagram \fIFILE\fR
Also write a zone/conduit diagram to this file. Format controlled by
\fB\-\-diagram\-format\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-diagram\-format " " {mermaid,dot}
Diagram syntax for \fB\-\-diagram\fR: a Mermaid \fBgraph LR\fR block, or a
Graphviz \fB.dot\fR \fBdigraph\fR. Default: mermaid.
.TP
.B \-\-policy\-out \fIFILE\fR
Also write the inferred zone/conduit model as a \fBpolicy\fR\-format YAML
file, directly loadable by \fBpolicy validate \-\-policy\fR \-\- closing the
loop: discover, then enforce. If no asset was observed at all, the file
contains only explanatory comments, no \fBzones:\fR/\fBconduits:\fR keys
(not a validly-loadable policy as-is), and a note is printed saying so.
.TP
.B \-\-mac\-vendor
Same meaning as \fBdecode\fR's \fB\-\-mac\-vendor\fR, applied to the report's
asset/edge MAC addresses. Off by default.
.TP
.B \-\-resolve
Same meaning as \fBdecode\fR's \fB\-\-resolve\fR: enable hostname resolution
from an explicitly-supplied \fB\-\-hosts\fR file. Off by default.
File-only \-\- \fBnever\fR performs live DNS.
.TP
.B \-\-hosts \fIFILE\fR
Unix /etc/hosts-style file to resolve IP addresses from, for
\fB\-\-resolve\fR. Must exist.
.TP
.B \-\-nn
Same meaning as \fBdecode\fR's \fB\-\-nn\fR: disable service name
(port\->name) resolution, applied to each edge's server port. On by
default.
.TP
.B \-\-services \fIFILE\fR
Unix /etc/services-style file to supplement/override the built-in
port\->service-name table. Must exist.
.SH LIVE CAPTURE
\fB\-i/\-\-interface\fR and \fBinterfaces\fR require this build to have been
compiled with libpcap (Linux) or the Npcap SDK (Windows) found by CMake --
an optional, build-time-detected dependency, the one exception to
conduitscope's otherwise zero-dependency design. A build without it is
otherwise unaffected; \fB\-i\fR/\fBinterfaces\fR just report that clearly at
runtime instead of not existing.
.PP
A live capture stops -- decoding/reporting whatever was captured so far,
same as reaching the end of an offline file -- on \fB\-\-duration\fR
elapsing, \fB\-\-max\-packets\fR being reached (\fBdecode\fR only), or
Ctrl+C (SIGINT on Linux; a console Ctrl+C event via
\fBSetConsoleCtrlHandler\fR on Windows, not the portable
\fBsignal(SIGINT, ...)\fR API, which isn't reliable enough there for this),
which is caught and used to stop cleanly rather than killing the process
outright. If colorized output is active, the terminal's colors are reset
twice on the way out, regardless of which of the three conditions above
stopped the capture: immediately from the Ctrl+C handler itself, as a
best-effort backstop in case the process is killed before exiting normally;
and again, authoritatively, right before \fBdecode\fR actually exits, which
is the one guaranteed to be the last byte written. The immediate reset alone
isn't enough on Windows, where the Ctrl+C handler runs on its own
Windows-spawned thread genuinely concurrently with the thread still decoding
and printing packets -- a capture can legitimately hand back one more
already-buffered packet after Ctrl+C, and that packet's buffered colored
output can reach the terminal after the handler's own reset, undoing it.
The authoritative end-of-run reset has no such race on any platform.
.PP
Ctrl+C during \fB\-r\fR (an offline file, not \fB\-i\fR) is caught the same
way and for the same reason -- colorized output needs the same clean reset
regardless of which kind of packet source is being read -- and it now also
actually stops the read early, the same as it does for a live capture,
rather than continuing to the end of the file regardless.
.PP
Promiscuous mode is on by default (\fB\-\-no\-promiscuous\fR turns it off)
because the primary use case -- watching a mirrored/SPAN switch port for
zone/conduit traffic between two other devices -- needs to see traffic not
addressed to the capturing host at all.
.PP
On Windows, install the Npcap SDK to build with live-capture support, and
the separate Npcap runtime/driver on whatever machine actually runs a
conduitscope binary built with it -- these are two different installs. See
\fBdocs/USER_GUIDE.md\fR's LIVE CAPTURE section for the full reference,
including why this has only been validated on Linux so far.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress non-essential diagnostic/warning output.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable ANSI color in \fBdecode\fR's text\-format output. Mutually exclusive
with \fB\-\-color\fR. See \fBCOLORIZED OUTPUT\fR below.
.TP
.B \-\-color
Force ANSI color in \fBdecode\fR's text\-format output, even when not
writing directly to a terminal (e.g. piping to a pager that understands
color, like \fBless \-R\fR). Mutually exclusive with \fB\-\-no\-color\fR.
See \fBCOLORIZED OUTPUT\fR below.
.TP
.B \-\-log\-file \fIFILE\fR
Write diagnostic/warning messages to \fIFILE\fR instead of stderr.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit. Available at every command level.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version, compiler, platform, and build type, then exit.
.SH COLORIZED OUTPUT
Applies to \fBdecode\fR's \fBtext\fR format only (not \fBjson\fR/\fBcsv\fR,
and not \fBpolicy validate\fR's report, which stays plain text by design --
it's meant to be diffed/archived/piped as an audit artifact). The
\fB[protocol]\fR tag is colored per protocol: cyan for Modbus, magenta for
DNP3, green for IEC 104, blue for the generic recognized\-but\-not\-decoded
COTP fallback (a TPKT/COTP frame that isn't S7comm/S7comm\-Plus/MMS
underneath \-\- e.g. connection setup/teardown), Siemens' own official brand
teal (\fB#009999\fR, "Viridian Green"/Petrol, Pantone 7716 C, RAL 5018,
their brand color since 1991) for S7comm, the same teal but bold for
S7comm\-Plus (the "same vendor's two generations" relationship this
tool's color scheme already expressed between plain S7comm and bold MMS,
just now with S7comm/S7comm\-Plus sharing the teal family instead), and
bold blue for MMS (kept at its own established hue rather than following
S7comm into teal \-\- MMS is a distinct IEC 61850 application protocol, not
one of Siemens' own S7 product line, despite sharing S7comm's exact
TPKT/COTP transport/port), Beckhoff's own brand red (\fB#E2001A\fR, a
reasonable approximation \-\- no single authoritative digital hex value was
found for it) for TwinCAT/ADS, yellow for
EtherNet/IP,
bright cyan for PROFINET RT, bright green for GOOSE, bright magenta for
Sampled Values, bright yellow for EtherCAT, plain magenta for STP
(deliberately shares DNP3's own plain magenta \-\- the two never share a
transport/link at all, STP being classic 802.3 LLC framing rather than
IP-based, so there is no realistic capture where this collision would
actually confuse a reader scanning by eye), bright blue for BACnet/IP,
bright white for HART-IP, bright red for OPC UA, bold cyan for MQTT
(deliberately close to Modbus's plain cyan, but bold and otherwise distinct
from every other tag color -- MQTT shares no transport/port with any other
decoded protocol), bright red for FF-HSE too (deliberately shares OPC UA's
own bright red rather than adding a 20th distinct hue -- the two never
share a transport/port, so there is no realistic capture where this
collision would actually confuse a reader scanning by eye), bold green for
DeviceNet (a genuinely new hue -- DeviceNet is the only protocol here that
isn't Ethernet-based at all, so a mixed-protocol capture containing it is
structurally impossible, no collision risk to reason about either way),
plain white shared by DNS/mDNS/LLMNR (the exact same wire format, see
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's section -- the \fB[protocol]\fR tag
text itself is what disambiguates them, not the color), gray for NBT-NS (a
fresh hue, since NBT-NS traffic routinely coexists with DNS-family and
everything else in a real mixed IT/OT capture), and bold yellow for DoH
(also fresh, since DoH detection fires on ordinary TCP/443 traffic that can
appear alongside literally any other protocol here), dim
for everything else
recognized but not OT\-specific. A Modbus exception response's summary, and a
\fBparse\-error\fR packet's whole line, are bold red. Notes are dim.
.PP
Color is used only when writing directly to an interactive terminal by
default \-\- never into a file via \fB\-o\fR, and never when piped. Force it
on with \fB\-\-color\fR (e.g. to pipe into \fBless \-R\fR) or off with
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR; the two are mutually exclusive.
.PP
Every tag color above is one of the 16 standard ANSI colors except S7comm's,
S7comm\-Plus's, and TwinCAT/ADS's own teal/red, which use 24\-bit "truecolor"
escape codes to match their vendors' actual brand colors -- a terminal
without truecolor support may render these three as the nearest color it
has, or not at all, rather than the intended hue.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. For \fBpolicy validate\fR: the capture is COMPLIANT. For
\fBinventory\fR: a report was produced \-\- it has no compliance concept, so
this is returned on any successful run, even one that observed zero assets.
.TP
.B 1
Fatal error (bad arguments, unreadable/unrecognized input file, a
\fB\-\-strict\fR parse failure, or, for \fBpolicy validate\fR, a policy file
that couldn't be opened or failed validation).
.TP
.B 2
Currently unused. Reserved rather than reused: an earlier release used this
for \fBpolicy validate\fR while it was still a documented stub.
.TP
.B 3
\fBpolicy validate\fR only: the capture and policy were both valid, but the
capture is NON-COMPLIANT (at least one violation and/or unclassified flow),
OR \fB\-\-strict\-it\-protocols\fR was given and the report's "notable
protocols" finding is non\-empty. Never returned by \fBinventory\fR.
.SH LIMITATIONS
A handful of rare/obsolete pcapng block types (the pre-2000s "Packet Block",
Interface Statistics Blocks, Name Resolution Blocks, Decryption Secrets
Blocks, and custom/vendor block types) are skipped rather than decoded --
no mainstream capture tool writes these, so this essentially never matters
in practice; general TCP stream reassembly is implemented but
narrowly scoped: a Modbus PDU, a single DNP3 data-link frame's own
header/blocks, an IEC 104 APDU, an EtherNet/IP encapsulation message, or a
TPKT/COTP frame split across TCP segments IS
reassembled (per directional TCP flow, using each protocol's own declared
length); authoritative (transaction-ID + TCP-session, non-heuristic) Modbus
request/response pairing is layered on top of the always-on payload-shape
heuristic, but only pairs a response when its request is actually present
on the same TCP session in this capture; a genuine S7comm message chained
across multiple complete TPKT/COTP frames via COTP's own EOT bit is also
reassembled, though real-capture validation of that path so far only
confirms it is transparent on content-free "priming" fragments, not genuine
multi-frame content splitting (synthetic-fixture-validated only); IPv4
only; no IPv4 fragment reassembly; DNP3's
own 16-bit data-link source/destination station addresses (NOT IP
addresses) are exposed as \fIdnp3_source_address\fR/
\fIdnp3_destination_address\fR, the actual outstation/master identity a
serial-to-IP gateway multiplexes behind one shared IP, but not (yet)
consulted by the zone/conduit engine; DNP3
header and per-block CRCs are now validated (\fIdnp3_link_crc_valid\fR and
related JSON fields, plus a \fBnotes\fR entry on mismatch), diagnostic only
-- a mismatch never stops decoding and is not (yet) evaluated by
\fBpolicy validate\fR; a DNP3 application fragment
spanning more than one data-link frame IS reassembled across separate TCP
segments (per TCP flow), though this path has no real-capture validation
yet; DNP3 point values are decoded
only for the group/variation combinations in the built-in point-format
table (an object header outside it is still located and skipped by
computed length); IEC 104 needs no
cross-frame reassembly (one I-format APDU always carries exactly one ASDU)
but its ASDU type-ID decode table, like DNP3's, covers only a "first pass"
set of common monitoring/command/setpoint type IDs -- an ASDU outside that
set is still identified by type name and structurally located, just not
value-decoded; EtherNet/IP's CIP explicit-message value decoding covers a
"first pass" set of generic common/Connection-Manager services plus,
specifically when the request path addresses a Rockwell Logix5000 named
tag (not a class/instance path), the Symbol-object tag services -- a
class/instance-addressed request reusing one of those same service codes
is shown structurally instead, and NOP (command 0x0000) is deliberately
not recognized as a command at all (see \fBdocs/USER_GUIDE.md\fR's LIMITATIONS
and \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR); EtherNet/IP CIP I/O (implicit messaging, UDP port
2222) is now decoded too, but only its Sequenced Address Item (connection
ID + sequence number) is value-decoded -- the Connected Data Item's actual
I/O data is shown only as raw hex, never value-decoded (no generic
self-describing type, and this decoder doesn't track a connection's
negotiated transport class), datagrams are decoded independently with no
cross-datagram correlation to the Forward_Open that established a
connection, and no real capture was found to validate this decoder against
(synthetic-fixture-validated only); PROFINET RT (EtherType 0x8892) is also
now decoded -- DCP (device discovery/configuration: full PDU header, block
list, five value-decoded block types) is validated against two real
captures, one of which caught a genuine decoding bug (a BlockInfo/
BlockQualifier 2-byte prefix on certain DCP blocks) before this feature
ever shipped; cyclic real-time IO datagrams are decoded too (IO data plus
the CycleCounter/DataStatus/TransferStatus trailer), but the IO data's
length is inferred, not read from a field (indistinguishable from
Ethernet minimum-frame padding on a short datagram), no real capture with
cyclic IO data was found to validate that path against, and a DCP Xid is
never used to authoritatively pair a request to its response; IEC 61850-8-1
GOOSE (EtherType 0x88B8) is also now decoded -- every IECGoosePdu field
(gocbRef/datSet/goID/timestamp/stNum/sqNum/confRev/simulation/ndsCom) and
every allData value type (booleans, bit-strings, integers, floating point,
strings, timestamps, and nested array/structure values, recursively) --
validated against four real captures totaling 494 real GOOSE frames, but
every one of those frames happens to share the same narrow shape (every
optional field present, allData values only ever boolean/bit-string), so
optional-field absence, every other allData type, a GSE Management PDU
(the outer APDU's other, undecoded shape), and a truncated/malformed frame
are all validated only against a hand-built synthetic fixture, not an
independent real capture; IEC 61850-9-2 Sampled Values (EtherType 0x88BA,
GOOSE's sibling protocol) is also now decoded -- every ASDU field
(svID/datSet/smpCnt/confRev/refrTm/smpSynch/smpRate/smpMod/gmidData) except
seqData, the sample payload itself, which is deliberately shown only as raw
hex and never value-decoded (no generic self-describing type, same
reasoning as CIP I/O's Connected Data Item above); unlike GOOSE, every ASDU
in a frame's seqASDU is decoded, not just the first; despite a genuine
multi-source search, no real Sampled Values capture was found anywhere, so
this decoder is validated only against a hand-built synthetic fixture,
covering every field, both enumerated fields' full value sets, multi-ASDU
framing, and every malformed/truncated path; EtherCAT (EtherType 0x88A4,
plain fixed-binary-layout, no ASN.1/BER) is also now decoded -- the 2-byte
frame header (Length/Reserved/Type) and, for Type 1 ("EtherCAT command")
frames, the chained EtherCAT datagram(s) (Cmd/Idx/Adp-Ado-or-logical-address/
Len+flags/Irq/Data/Working Counter) -- Data is deliberately never
value-decoded, same reasoning as SV's seqData above; its frame-header Type
field is a genuinely weaker structural detection signal than this tool's
other raw-Ethernet decoders (5 of 16 possible values, vs. GOOSE/SV's
1-in-256 outer tag), so the dedicated EtherType remains the primary
confidence source; validated against a genuine 986-frame real capture (zero
declared-Length-vs-actual-bytes mismatches across all 986 frames, confirming
the chain-bounding design), but APRW/FPRW/BRW/LRW/ARMW/FRMW/EXT/NOP, the
Circulating bit, VLAN tagging, and non-Type-1 frames are validated only
against a hand-built synthetic fixture; BACnet/IP (UDP port 47808/0xBAC0,
detected by payload shape, not port) is also now decoded -- BVLC framing
(including every BBMD/foreign-device-table management function), NPDU
(including DEST/SRC routing and Network Layer Messages, named only), and
APDU (all 8 PDU types, byte-accurate including segmentation) are decoded,
but service value-decoding is a deliberate "first pass" covering only
Who-Is/I-Am/Who-Has/I-Have device discovery and ReadProperty/
WriteProperty/generic-Error -- every other service is named only, shown as
raw hex, as is a constructed/array PropertyValue and a segmented APDU's
own service data (no cross-packet reassembly); validated against a genuine
54-frame real capture (Original-Unicast-NPDU ReadProperty traffic against
trend-log objects), but device discovery, WriteProperty, every
PropertyValue type besides a scalar Unsigned, every other BVLC function,
and NPDU routing fields are validated only against a hand-built synthetic
fixture; HART-IP (UDP/TCP port 5094, IEC 62591 / HCF_SPEC-151) is also now
decoded -- the 8-byte HART-IP header, all four MessageID-selected body
shapes (Session Initiate, Session Close, Keep Alive, Pass Through), and,
inside Pass-Through, the full wired-HART Data-Link PDU (Delimiter/Address/
Command/Byte-Count/Response-Code/Device-Status/Data/Checksum) -- but its
own structural detection gate (an 8-byte header with only loose value
checks) is the weakest of any protocol in this tool, and is a KNOWN,
ACCEPTED, DOCUMENTED LIMITATION: on TCP it collides with Modbus/TCP's own
MBAP-header detection on certain byte patterns (reordering the two was
tried and rejected -- it regressed ~2% of this tool's own Modbus/TCP test
corpus -- so HART-IP is deliberately tried last), and, confirmed on a
genuine real capture, the same weak gate also false-positives against
unrelated background TCP traffic (TLS, SMB) that has nothing to do with
either protocol; the Data-Link Checksum IS now verified (computed and
compared against the wire byte, a mismatch flagged as its own note), unlike
S7comm-Plus's own Checksums/digests, which are still surfaced raw only;
the Device-Status byte's flags are decoded but the header's own Status byte
is, per the spec itself, always 0 in current usage; commands 31 and 203 are
decoded structurally but deliberately given no authoritative top-level name
(no reliable source names either, only an inferred pairing); command
value-decoding is a "first pass" covering the most common read/write/
identification commands, with commands 77 and 178 in particular named but
not value-decoded; and validation is against both a comprehensive synthetic
fixture and a genuine 116-frame real capture, but that real capture only
exercises 9 of the roughly 20 decoded commands, no Error/NAK messages, no
BACK frame type, and no non-Success response codes (see
\fBdocs/USER_GUIDE.md\fR's LIMITATIONS section and
\fBtests/real_captures/hartip/ATTRIBUTION.md\fR for the full detail); OPC UA
Binary (TCP port 4840, UA-TCP transport / OPC UA Secure Conversation, tried
FIRST of all protocols due to its own strong, collision-free structural
detection gate) is also now decoded -- the 8-byte UA-TCP common header, the
Secure Conversation security/sequence headers, and full field-level
("Tier 1") decode of the connection-lifecycle and discovery services
(Hello/Acknowledge/Error/ReverseHello, OpenSecureChannel, GetEndpoints,
FindServers, CreateSession, ActivateSession -- including, deliberately, the
cleartext Password field of a UserName/Password identity token sent over an
unencrypted channel, flagged as a SECURITY FINDING) -- and OPC UA's own
Variant/DataValue self-describing value encoding IS now implemented, which
promoted Read/Write/Call to full ("Tier 1") decode too, so their own
process/tag values (ReadResponse's Results, WriteRequest's NodesToWrite,
Call's Input/Output Arguments) are genuinely decoded, not just named;
Browse and every subscription/MonitoredItem-management service, plus
HistoryRead, are still named with their RequestHeader/ResponseHeader
decoded only, their own bodies shown as raw hex, since none of the former
actually carries a Variant/DataValue in its own body and the latter needs
additional scope of its own; a logical message split across multiple
'C'/'F' OPC UA chunks (distinct from ordinary TCP-segment reassembly, which
IS implemented) is not reassembled, only a single complete 'F' chunk's body
is decoded; there is no stateful channel/session tracking (a TokenId or
AuthenticationToken is never correlated back to the exchange that issued
it); the StatusCode table is a first-pass ~20 entries out of the OPC
Foundation's own ~700, with every other value still correctly decoding its
Good/Uncertain/Bad severity from the raw hex; and real-capture validation,
while genuine (two independent-stack sessions, 9 of ~21 Tier 1
request/response entries, real multi-segment TCP reassembly), never
exercises FindServers, CloseSession, CloseSecureChannel, any Tier 2
service, a non-Anonymous identity token, a non-'F' chunk, a
structurally-invalid NodeId, or a well-formed (non-fuzzed) Read/Write/Call
exchange -- both real CallRequest bodies in that capture turn out to be
genuinely malformed Achilles-Satellite fuzz-test payloads, which this
decoder's own bounds-checked reads now correctly reject (see
\fBdocs/USER_GUIDE.md\fR's LIMITATIONS section
and \fBtests/real_captures/opcua/ATTRIBUTION.md\fR for the full detail);
MQTT (v3.1/v3.1.1/v5.0, conventionally TCP port 1883) and, layered on top
of it, Sparkplug B are also now decoded \-\- but MQTT's own structural
detection gate (no fixed magic number or length field) is honestly the
weakest in this codebase, which is why it's dispatched dead last (see
\fB\-\-protocol\fR above); CONNECT/CONNACK/PUBLISH/SUBSCRIBE/SUBACK/
UNSUBSCRIBE/UNSUBACK/PINGREQ/PINGRESP/DISCONNECT/AUTH are all field-decoded
across all three versions, with MQTT5 Properties fully parsed, and
CONNECT's cleartext username/password fields are decoded as a deliberate
SECURITY FINDING (this protocol has no transport encryption of its own);
because SUBSCRIBE/SUBACK/UNSUBSCRIBE's own wire shapes are ambiguous
between MQTT versions, each TCP session's version is tracked from its own
CONNECT packet rather than guessed per-packet where a CONNECT was actually
seen, but a session with no CONNECT in this capture (or a SUBACK alone,
with the weakest evidence of any packet type) still falls back to a
per-packet heuristic; QoS 2 (exactly-once) delivery state, retained
messages, and the Will message's own delivery are not tracked across
packets, only decoded where they appear structurally; Sparkplug B's
protobuf-encoded payloads (NBIRTH/NDEATH/DBIRTH/DDEATH/DDATA/NDATA) are
decoded via a hand-rolled Protocol Buffers reader (no external protobuf
dependency), including the typed Metric value union and nested
Template/DataSet metric types, but the STATE message topic's own JSON
payload is shown only as raw text, not parsed; a real capture (a genuine
Eclipse Paho client's MQTT 3.1 traffic) was found and validated, and it
immediately surfaced a real bug \-\- MQTT 3.1's own session-version
tracking was silently never learned at all, only 3.1.1/5.0 were \-\- found
and fixed; despite a genuine search, no real Sparkplug B capture was
found, so Sparkplug B decoding is validated only against a hand-built
synthetic fixture (see \fBdocs/USER_GUIDE.md\fR's LIMITATIONS section and
\fBtests/real_captures/mqtt/ATTRIBUTION.md\fR for the full detail);
FOUNDATION Fieldbus HSE (FF-HSE, ports 1089/1090/1091/3622, TCP and UDP) is
also now decoded \-\- the 12-byte common header, its Options/trailer
bitmask fields, the great majority of FDA/SM/LAN Redundancy messages, and
FMS's own session-lifecycle/status/identify/read/write family, all sourced
from Wireshark's own vendor-authored \fBpacket-ff.c\fR dissector (no free
copy of FF-HSE's own paywalled FieldComm Group specs was available) \-\-
but its own structural detection gate (a single byte at header offset 2)
is honestly the WEAKEST in this whole codebase, so it is dispatched dead
LAST of all protocols, on both TCP and UDP; FMS's Get OD, Define/Delete
Variable List, the Download/Upload and Program Invocation families, and
unconfirmed Event Notification are named only, shown as raw hex (FMS Get
OD is left undecoded even by the reference dissector itself); FMS
Read/Write values are likewise always left as raw hex, since an FMS value
has no self-describing wire type without external Object Dictionary
context; the SM Identify Rsp / SM Device Annunciation LinkId branch (whose
trailing version-number list's own internal shape depends on a LinkId
value computed from the header, not the body) is this decoder's single
trickiest piece; whether cyclic Publisher/Subscriber traffic reuses the
unconfirmed FMS Information Report family is flagged everywhere as this
decoder's own inference, never asserted as fact; Network Management
(FF-803) and full HSE Redundancy beyond basic LAN Redundancy Get/Put/
Diagnostic are entirely out of scope; and despite a genuine multi-source
search, no real FF-HSE capture was found anywhere, so validation is
against a hand-built synthetic fixture only (see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's FOUNDATION Fieldbus HSE section and LIMITATIONS section
for the full detail);
IEEE Spanning Tree Protocol (STP/RSTP/MSTP, no port at all \-\- classic
IEEE 802.3 length-framed Ethernet, LLC DSAP=SSAP=0x42) is also now decoded
\-\- classic Configuration BPDUs, TCN BPDUs, RST BPDUs (RSTP/MSTP's shared
36-byte shape), and the full MST BPDU extension (every MSTI Configuration
Message, plus the reference source's own Cisco-C3550-firmware Version-3-
Length-below-64 work-around) \-\- but Cisco PVST+/Rapid-PVST+ (a different,
SNAP-encapsulated envelope), SPB (802.1aq), and the legacy/alternative
MSTI Configuration Message format (Version 3 Length == 0) are all named
only, not decoded; GARP (GVRP/GMRP, which shares STP's own LLC DSAP/SSAP
pair) is disambiguated purely by destination MAC \-\- the one case in this
codebase where a fixed address genuinely gates detection rather than a
structural check; validated against two real captures (42 classic
Configuration BPDUs, 12 real RSTP RST BPDUs), but neither exercises a TCN,
MSTP, SPB, the Proposal/Agreement flags, or a non-Designated Port Role on
real bytes \-\- those paths are validated only against the synthetic
\fBtests/sample_stp.pcap\fR fixture (see \fBdocs/USER_GUIDE.md\fR's PROTOCOL
COVERAGE Spanning Tree Protocol section and
\fBtests/real_captures/stp/ATTRIBUTION.md\fR for the full detail);
DeviceNet (CAN-bus CIP, a wholly separate pcap link type from Ethernet \-\-
\fILINKTYPE_CAN_SOCKETCAN\fR) is also now decoded \-\- CAN 11-bit standard
identifiers classified into 4 message groups (I/O data, master/scanner
commands plus Duplicate-MAC-ID-Check, unconnected explicit CIP messaging,
offline/fault notifications), Group 3's CIP explicit-message service codes
reusing EtherNet/IP's own \fBcip_service_name\fR plus DeviceNet's own four
extra codes \-\- but extended (29-bit) IDs, RTR, and error frames are
rejected as not valid DeviceNet at all, CAN FD frames are recognized but
not semantically decoded, and Group 3 fragmentation is flagged but not
reassembled, matching Wireshark's own \fBpacket-devicenet.c\fR's own
unimplemented TODO, not a gap unique to this port; no real public
DeviceNet/CAN-bus capture could be found anywhere, so validation is
against the synthetic \fBtests/sample_devicenet.pcap\fR fixture only (see
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's DeviceNet section); ControlNet,
the other CIP-family network, is out of reach of this or any pcap-based
tool entirely (proprietary Manchester-coded coax physical layer, no
Wireshark dissector, no pcap link type \-\- see that same section's "Why
not ControlNet too" note), a structural limitation, not a tracked gap;
non-IPv4
Ethernet frames and non-TCP
IPv4 payloads (including UDP) are otherwise still just recognized and
NAMED for a small OT-relevant set (ARP, LLDP, ICMP, and the rest) but not further decoded, and
\fBpolicy validate\fR does not yet evaluate any UDP traffic (CIP I/O,
BACnet/IP, HART-IP's own UDP traffic, and FF-HSE's own UDP traffic
included) against a conduit \-\- this carries through to \fBinventory\fR
too: it infers CIP I/O/BACnet-IP conduits from observed UDP traffic and
\fB\-\-policy\-out\fR's generated policy file loads them without error, but
feeding that file back into \fBpolicy validate\fR always reports them as
"never exercised," regardless of how much matching UDP traffic the capture
has; a zone can instead be declared as a set of
VLAN IDs (\fBvlans:\fR) rather than IPv4 CIDR blocks, and a conduit
referencing only VLAN zones IS evaluated against PROFINET RT/GOOSE/Sampled
Values/EtherCAT traffic (the four protocols with no IP layer at all),
classified by whether the frame's own 802.1Q tag falls within a declared
VLAN zone \-\- see \fBdocs/USER_GUIDE.md\fR's POLICY FILE FORMAT "Addressing
scope" section. A VLAN-zone conduit's \fBfrom\fR/\fBto\fR must name the
exact same zone set (a single frame carries at most one VLAN tag, so
there's no separate source/destination zone), and it can't use
\fBports\fR, \fBbidirectional: true\fR, or \fBfunctions\fR/\fBfunction\fR
\-\- none of those has a meaning for this traffic. Only a single, ordinary
802.1Q tag is ever consulted: a stacked/QinQ frame (EtherType 0x88A8) is
never unwrapped, so it isn't even decoded as PROFINET RT/GOOSE/SV/EtherCAT
in the first place, let alone VLAN-zone-classified;
Modbus request/response
classification falls back to a payload-shape heuristic only when
authoritative pairing can't be done (no matching request on this session);
S7comm item-level addressing (inside Read Var / Write Var) is fully
confident only for the classic S7ANY syntax; the S7-1200/1500 "symbolic"
syntax (0xB2) gets an EXPERIMENTAL, unverified decode, marked
[EXPERIMENTAL] everywhere it appears; PLC Control's (0x28) _N_*
Sinumerik/CNC PI services get a name+description lookup only, never a
parameter-block decode, and P_PROGRAM/_MODU/_GARB argument strings are
shown raw, never semantically interpreted;
S7comm-Plus (TCP port 102, shares S7comm's own TPKT/COTP transport,
disambiguated by its own protocol id byte 0x72) is now decoded too, with the
same two-tier posture as MMS/OPC UA: GetMultiVariables, SetMultiVariables,
SetVariable, and DeleteObject (the traffic that dominates real S7comm-Plus
captures) are fully decoded in both directions, including the protocol's own
native symbolic item addressing (a CRC-like hash plus a chain of "LID"
struct/array-member values -- genuinely native, unlike S7comm's own
EXPERIMENTAL 0xB2 reconstruction, though a LID's/CRC's symbolic meaning is
inherently unresolvable without TIA Portal's own project database) and its
self-describing "Value" encoding (20+ datatypes, including recursively
nested STRUCT values); CreateObject, Explore, GetLink, BeginSequence/
EndSequence, Invoke, GetVarSubStreamed, Notification, and Connect are
recognized (named) but not body-decoded, and DataFW1_5 (firmware >= V1.5)
gets header-only decode, since this decoder could not independently confirm
the reference plugin's own byte-accounting for where that variant's body
starts; S7comm-Plus's own above-COTP, trailer-based fragmentation (distinct
from COTP's own EOT bit) is detected and reported but not reassembled; an
array of Struct values is a real but rare shape this decoder deliberately
refuses to decode rather than risk silent misalignment; validated against
two real S7-1511 captures with zero per-item decode errors -- see
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's S7comm-Plus section and
\fBtests/real_captures/s7comm/ATTRIBUTION.md\fR's own S7comm-Plus addendum;
IEC 61850 MMS (TCP port 102, shares S7comm's own TPKT/COTP transport) is
also now decoded \-\- the full Session/Presentation/ACSE/MMS layer stack,
including, unlike this tool's own OPC UA decoder, MMS's own self-describing
"Data" value type (14 of 17 alternatives, depth-capped at 32 levels against
a real recursion-assertion denial-of-service bug this decoder's own
validation found in tshark 4.2.2's own MMS dissector) \-\- but only 18 of
MMS's 78 confirmedServices (status, getNameList, identify, read, write,
getVariableAccessAttributes, defineNamedVariableList,
getNamedVariableListAttributes, deleteNamedVariableList,
getDomainAttributes, getCapabilityList, and the seven file-transfer
services obtainFile, fileOpen, fileRead, fileClose, fileRename, fileDelete,
fileDirectory \-\- IEC 61850's own COMTRADE/disturbance-file-retrieval
workflow rides on exactly these seven) get full field decoding, the rest
are name + invokeID only with the body shown as raw hex; within the
file-transfer group, \fBObtainFile-Request\fR's own \fBsourceFileServer\fR
(\fBApplicationReference\fR) is likewise recognized but not deep-decoded;
the Session
layer's extended (2-byte) length form is not supported, only the "normal"
one-byte-length-per-parameter form; \fBAddress\fR (non-symbolic variable
addressing) and \fBTypeSpecification\fR are recognized but not decoded;
this decoder assumes the near-universal "1=ACSE, 3=MMS"
presentation-context convention for an ongoing message with no
context-list of its own, corroborated by every real capture checked but an
assumption, not a guarantee; and validation is against three small genuine
real-world captures plus a 224-frame capture this project generated itself
against a real independent stack (\fBmz-automation/libiec61850\fR),
117 of 224 frames recognized, zero parse warnings (see
\fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's MMS section and
\fBtests/real_captures/mms/ATTRIBUTION.md\fR for the full detail, including
two genuine structural-detection-gate bugs this validation pass caught and
fixed);
ICCP/TASE.2 (IEC 60870-6-802) traffic \-\- which rides this exact same MMS
stack rather than a distinct wire protocol \-\- is recognized by its own
reserved system-variable-name vocabulary (\fBBilateral_Table_ID\fR,
\fBTASE2_Version\fR, \fBSupported_Features\fR, and others) and flagged with
a curated note, including a distinct higher-value note for a write to a
Select-Before-Operate (\fB_SBO\fR) or tag (\fB_TAG\fR) device-control
variable, but stays labeled and counted as \fBmms\fR rather than a
separate protocol \-\- see \fBdocs/PROTOCOL_COVERAGE.md\fR's ICCP/TASE.2
subsection for the full design, sourcing, and this first pass's own
honestly-stated validation gap (no independently buildable TASE.2 stack
was available to generate real wire traffic against);
live capture is an optional,
build-time-detected feature (see LIVE CAPTURE) whose Windows/Npcap path has
not yet been run on a real Windows machine, validated on Linux so far only
against loopback traffic;
\fBpolicy validate\fR's zones are IPv4 CIDR-only and its client/server
(initiator) determination falls back to a port-number heuristic when no
SYN/SYN-ACK was captured for a flow. See the
"LIMITATIONS" section of \fBdocs/USER_GUIDE.md\fR and the "ROADMAP"
section of \fBdocs/DEVELOPMENT.md\fR in the source tree for the full,
current detail and what's planned next.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Decode a capture as text:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap
.EE
.RE
.PP
Force color into a pager (color is off by default once piped):
.RS
.EX
conduitscope \-\-color decode \-r capture.pcap | less \-R
.EE
.RE
.PP
Get file metadata and a protocol histogram:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope info \-r capture.pcap
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decode as JSON and filter with jq:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-f json | jq '.[] | select(.protocol=="modbus")'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decode IEC 61850 MMS traffic and show every read/write/report value:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol mms \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '.[] | select(.mms_values) | "\e(.src_ip) \-> \e(.dst_ip): \e(.mms_values | join(", "))"'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decode S7comm-Plus (TIA Portal S7-1200/1500) traffic and show every
read/write value:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol s7comm\-plus \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '.[] | select(.s7plus_values) | "\e(.src_ip) \-> \e(.dst_ip): \e(.s7plus_values | join(", "))"'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Summarize IEC 104 ASDUs (type and cause of transmission) from a JSON decode:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol iec104 \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '.[] | select(.iec104_asdu_type) | "\e(.src_ip) \-> \e(.dst_ip): \e(.iec104_asdu_type) (\e(.iec104_cot))"'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Fingerprint EtherNet/IP devices that answered a ListIdentity request:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol enip \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '.[] | select((.enip_command == "ListIdentity") and (.summary | contains("identity:"))) | .src_ip'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Summarize CIP I/O (implicit messaging) traffic by connection:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol enip \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '[.[] | select(.enip_io_connection_id != null)] | group_by(.enip_io_connection_id) |
           .[] | "\e(.[0].enip_io_connection_id): \e(length) datagram(s)"'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Build a quick PROFINET device inventory from DCP exchanges:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol profinet \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '[.[] | select(.profinet_dcp_blocks != null) |
           .profinet_dcp_blocks[] | select(startswith("NameOfStation="))] | unique[]'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Build a quick GOOSE publisher inventory (gocbRef + datSet):
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol goose \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '[.[] | select(.goose_gocb_ref != null) | "\e(.goose_gocb_ref) (\e(.goose_dat_set))"] | unique[]'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Build a quick Sampled Values publisher/stream inventory (svID + smpRate):
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol sv \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '[.[] | select(.sv_id != null) | "\e(.sv_id) smpRate=\e(.sv_smp_rate // "n/a")"] | unique[]'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Spot an EtherCAT Working Counter anomaly (WKC=0, usually a slave that
didn't respond):
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol ethercat \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '.[] | select(.ethercat_first_wkc == 0) |
           "\e(.index): \e(.ethercat_first_cmd_name) adp=\e(.ethercat_first_adp // "n/a")"'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Build a quick BACnet device inventory from I-Am announcements:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol bacnet \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '[.[] | select(.bacnet_service_name == "i\-Am") | "\e(.src_ip): \e(.bacnet_values | join(", "))"] | unique[]'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Build a quick HART-IP device inventory (Unique Identifier responses by address):
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol hartip \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '[.[] | select(.hartip_command_name == "Read Unique Identifier" and .hartip_is_response == true) |
           "\e(.hartip_address): \e(.hartip_values | join(", "))"] | unique[]'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Build an OPC UA endpoint/security-posture inventory -- every distinct
endpoint offered by a server's own GetEndpointsResponse, useful for
spotting an endpoint still accepting SecurityMode "None":
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol opcua \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '[.[] | select(.opcua_service_name == "GetEndpointsResponse") | .opcua_values[] | select(startswith("endpoint["))] | unique[]'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Find every OPC UA ActivateSession request that placed a UserName/Password
credential on the wire in cleartext -- this decoder's own deliberate
"SECURITY FINDING" note, worth flagging on any conduit that should be
running an authenticated, encrypted OPC UA session:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol opcua \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '.[] | select(.notes // [] | any(startswith("SECURITY FINDING"))) |
           "\e(.src_ip):\e(.src_port) \-> \e(.dst_ip):\e(.dst_port): \e(.opcua_values[] | select(startswith("username=") or startswith("password=")))"'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Find MQTT CONNECT packets carrying cleartext credentials -- MQTT's own
Username/Password fields carry no confidentiality of their own, the same
deliberate "SECURITY FINDING" pattern as OPC UA's Identity Token check
above:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol mqtt \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '.[] | select(.mqtt_values // [] | any(startswith("Username=") or startswith("Password="))) |
           "\e(.src_ip):\e(.src_port) \-> \e(.dst_ip):\e(.dst_port): \e(.mqtt_values[] | select(startswith("Username=") or startswith("Password=")))"'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Pull out every decoded Sparkplug B metric across a capture, grouped by
group/edge-node/device:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol mqtt \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '.[] | select(.mqtt_is_sparkplug and (.mqtt_sparkplug_metrics // [] | length > 0)) |
           "\e(.mqtt_sparkplug_group_id)/\e(.mqtt_sparkplug_edge_node_id)\e(.mqtt_sparkplug_device_id // "" | if . == "" then "" else "/" + . end) \e(.mqtt_sparkplug_message_type): \e(.mqtt_sparkplug_metrics | join(", "))"'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Summarize FF-HSE traffic by sub-protocol and message name:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol ff\-hse \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '[.[] | select(.ffhse_protocol != null) | "\e(.ffhse_protocol) / \e(.ffhse_message_name)"] |
           group_by(.) | map("\e(length)x \e(.[0])") | .[]'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Pull every decoded FF-HSE field=value pair, alongside which message they
came from:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol ff\-hse \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '.[] | select(.ffhse_values // [] | length > 0) |
           "\e(.src_ip) \-> \e(.dst_ip): \e(.ffhse_message_name): \e(.ffhse_values | join(", "))"'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Build a quick STP bridge/root inventory (one line per distinct Bridge/Root
pair seen):
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol stp \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '[.[] | select(.stp_has_common_body) |
           "Bridge=\e(.stp_bridge_priority)/\e(.stp_bridge_sys_id_ext)/\e(.stp_bridge_mac) Root=\e(.stp_root_priority)/\e(.stp_root_sys_id_ext)/\e(.stp_root_mac)"] | unique[]'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Summarize a DeviceNet (CAN-bus) capture by message group and type:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol devicenet \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '[.[] | select(.protocol == "devicenet") | "\e(.devicenet_group_name) / \e(.devicenet_message_type)"] |
           group_by(.) | map("\e(length)x \e(.[0])") | .[]'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Surface TCP flows still stuck "buffering" a Modbus/TCP PDU that never
arrives -- the signature of HART-IP's own documented weak-detection-gate
collision (see LIMITATIONS above):
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '[.[] | select(.summary | test("buffering a (Modbus/TCP PDU|HART\-IP message)")) | .summary] | unique[]'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check a capture against a zone/conduit policy:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope policy validate \-r capture.pcap \-\-policy policy.yaml
.EE
.RE
.PP
Infer a first\-draft zone/conduit model from a capture, with a diagram, then
feed the generated policy straight back into \fBpolicy validate\fR \-\-
discover, then enforce:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope inventory \-r capture.pcap \-\-diagram zones.mmd \-\-policy\-out inferred.yaml
conduitscope policy validate \-r capture.pcap \-\-policy inferred.yaml
.EE
.RE
.PP
List capturable interfaces (requires live\-capture support):
.RS
.EX
conduitscope interfaces
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decode live traffic from eth0 for 60 seconds:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-i eth0 \-\-filter "port 502 or port 102 or port 5094 or port 4840 or port 1883" \-\-duration 60
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check live traffic against a policy until Ctrl+C:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope policy validate \-i eth0 \-\-policy policy.yaml
.EE
.RE
